const TOPICS = [
  // ─────────────────── JSON PARSER ──────────────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m13", "lld_m13_t5", 5, "Design a JSON Parser", "design-json-parser",
    ["case-study", "parsing", "recursive-descent", "state-machine"],
    "You are handed a byte stream that claims to be JSON. It may contain a surrogate-pair emoji written as two \\u escapes, a number like 1e309 that does not fit a double, a trailing comma a well-meaning human added, and — from an attacker — ten thousand nested open brackets. Your parser must produce a tree for the valid parts, a precise line-and-column error for the invalid ones, and must not die on the hostile one. Design a JSON Parser.",
    "The design is carried by ONE separation: a LEXER that turns characters into tokens and a recursive-descent PARSER that turns tokens into a tree, connected by exactly one token of lookahead. Character-level pain (escapes, unicode, number scanning) stays in the lexer; grammar-level pain (which production comes next) stays in the parser, and each of the two stays small enough to be correct. Everything that separates a toy from a real parser then lives in details you must name explicitly: surrogate-pair handling, number precision, an explicit DEPTH CAP so nesting cannot become a stack-overflow DoS, and errors that carry the position where the token started.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: accept a UTF-8 text input and produce an in-memory tree of JsonValue nodes covering the six JSON types — object, array, string, number, boolean, null — with objects preserving key order (a practical requirement even though the spec does not demand it, because round-tripping configuration files with reordered keys makes reviewers miserable). Reject anything not in the grammar and report WHERE: a message plus the line and column of the offending token, not merely 'invalid JSON'. Strings must decode the full escape set — quote, backslash, slash, backspace, formfeed, newline, carriage return, tab, and \\uXXXX — including SURROGATE PAIRS, so a four-byte emoji written as two escapes becomes one code point. Numbers must follow the JSON grammar exactly: optional minus, an integer part with no leading zeros, an optional fraction, an optional exponent; no NaN, no Infinity, no hex, no leading plus. Serialization back to text is a companion requirement worth mentioning but is much easier than parsing. Non-functional constraints do the design work. SAFETY: input is untrusted, so deeply nested arrays must not recurse the JVM stack into a StackOverflowError, and a giant literal must not allocate unboundedly — a parser is an attack surface and treating it as one is senior signal. STREAMING: the full tree may not fit in memory for large documents, so the token layer should be pull-based to leave a streaming/event API possible later. PERFORMANCE: one pass over the input, no substring-per-character allocation, no regular expressions on the hot path. Clarify scope out loud: strict RFC 8259 by default, with lenient extensions (comments, trailing commas) as explicit opt-in flags rather than silent tolerance."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "TOKEN is a small value object: a TokenType from a fixed enum (LBRACE, RBRACE, LBRACKET, RBRACKET, COLON, COMMA, STRING, NUMBER, TRUE, FALSE, NULL, EOF), the already-decoded payload for STRING and NUMBER tokens, and the LINE and COLUMN where the token began. Carrying position on the token — not on the parser — is what makes error messages point at the start of the bad construct instead of wherever the reader happened to stop. LEXER (or Scanner) owns the character cursor and every character-level rule: skipping whitespace, recognising literals, decoding string escapes into a StringBuilder, and scanning a number's characters into the exact substring the number grammar allows. It exposes a pull interface — next() returns the next Token — which is what keeps a streaming API on the table. PARSER owns the grammar and holds exactly ONE token of lookahead in a field, with two primitive helpers: peek() to inspect without consuming and expect(type) to consume-or-throw. JSONVALUE is the result hierarchy: JsonObject wrapping a LinkedHashMap, JsonArray wrapping a List, JsonString, JsonNumber, JsonBoolean, JsonNull — sealed in modern Java so exhaustive pattern matching over the tree is checkable by the compiler. PARSEEXCEPTION carries message plus line and column. DEPTH COUNTER is deliberately called out as an entity even though it is one int: it is incremented on entry to every nested value and compared against MAX_DEPTH, and its absence is the difference between a parser and a denial-of-service vector. A CONFIG object (strict vs lenient, max depth, max string length) keeps policy out of the algorithms."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The architecture is the classic two-stage compiler front end, and you should name it as such: lexical analysis then syntactic analysis, with a token stream as the interface between them. The payoff is not academic. Merged into one class, a JSON reader must simultaneously ask 'am I inside a string escape?' and 'am I expecting a value or a comma?' — two independent state machines interleaved in one method, which is exactly how real hand-rolled parsers rot into unmaintainable character soup. Split, the lexer is a flat switch on the current character with no grammar knowledge at all, and the parser never sees a backslash in its life. RECURSIVE DESCENT is the right parsing technique here and the reason is structural: JSON's grammar is small, LL(1) — one token of lookahead uniquely determines the production, because a value starting with a brace is an object, with a bracket an array, with a quote a string, and so on — and the grammar's recursion (objects contain values contain objects) maps one-to-one onto mutually recursive methods parseValue / parseObject / parseArray. The code and the grammar are readable side by side, which no table-driven parser generator achieves for a grammar this size. Patterns, honestly labelled: the JsonValue hierarchy is a COMPOSITE (containers and leaves share a type so trees are uniform), and a VISITOR over it serves serialization, pretty-printing, and schema validation without adding methods to every node — worth offering, though for six node types Java's sealed-interface pattern matching is the lazier, better-typed alternative and saying so shows judgment. What is deliberately absent: no regex (catastrophic backtracking on adversarial input is a real CVE class), no split-then-parse (allocates the whole document twice), and no parser generator (the grammar fits on a napkin)."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "enum TokType { LBRACE, RBRACE, LBRACKET, RBRACKET, COLON, COMMA,\n                STRING, NUMBER, TRUE, FALSE, NULL, EOF }\n\nrecord Token(TokType type, String text, double num, int line, int col) {}\n\nfinal class Lexer {\n  private final String src; private int i = 0, line = 1, col = 1;\n  Lexer(String src) { this.src = src; }\n\n  private char cur()  { return src.charAt(i); }\n  private void bump() { if (src.charAt(i) == '\\n') { line++; col = 1; } else col++; i++; }\n\n  Token next() {\n    while (i < src.length() && Character.isWhitespace(cur())) bump();\n    if (i >= src.length()) return new Token(TokType.EOF, \"\", 0, line, col);\n    int sl = line, sc = col;\n    char c = cur();\n    switch (c) {\n      case '{': bump(); return new Token(TokType.LBRACE, \"{\", 0, sl, sc);\n      case '}': bump(); return new Token(TokType.RBRACE, \"}\", 0, sl, sc);\n      case '[': bump(); return new Token(TokType.LBRACKET, \"[\", 0, sl, sc);\n      case ']': bump(); return new Token(TokType.RBRACKET, \"]\", 0, sl, sc);\n      case ':': bump(); return new Token(TokType.COLON, \":\", 0, sl, sc);\n      case ',': bump(); return new Token(TokType.COMMA, \",\", 0, sl, sc);\n      case '\"': return new Token(TokType.STRING, readString(), 0, sl, sc);\n      default:\n        if (c == '-' || (c >= '0' && c <= '9'))\n          return new Token(TokType.NUMBER, null, readNumber(), sl, sc);\n        return keyword(sl, sc);\n    }\n  }\n\n  private String readString() {                 // ALL escape pain lives here\n    bump();                                     // opening quote\n    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n    while (true) {\n      if (i >= src.length()) throw new ParseException(\"unterminated string\", line, col);\n      char c = cur();\n      if (c == '\"') { bump(); return sb.toString(); }\n      if (c < 0x20) throw new ParseException(\"raw control char in string\", line, col);\n      if (c != '\\\\') { sb.append(c); bump(); continue; }\n      bump();                                   // consume backslash\n      char e = cur(); bump();\n      switch (e) {\n        case '\"' -> sb.append('\"');   case '\\\\' -> sb.append('\\\\');\n        case '/' -> sb.append('/');   case 'b'  -> sb.append('\\b');\n        case 'f' -> sb.append('\\f');  case 'n'  -> sb.append('\\n');\n        case 'r' -> sb.append('\\r');  case 't'  -> sb.append('\\t');\n        case 'u' -> sb.append(readHex4());      // surrogate halves append as-is:\n        default  -> throw new ParseException(\"bad escape \\\\\" + e, line, col);\n      }                                         // a valid pair recomposes in UTF-16\n    }\n  }\n\n  private char readHex4() {\n    int v = 0;\n    for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++) {\n      int d = Character.digit(cur(), 16);\n      if (d < 0) throw new ParseException(\"bad \\\\u escape\", line, col);\n      v = v * 16 + d; bump();\n    }\n    return (char) v;\n  }\n\n  private double readNumber() {\n    int start = i;\n    if (cur() == '-') bump();\n    if (cur() == '0') bump();                   // no leading zeros allowed\n    else while (i < src.length() && Character.isDigit(cur())) bump();\n    if (i < src.length() && cur() == '.') { bump();\n      while (i < src.length() && Character.isDigit(cur())) bump(); }\n    if (i < src.length() && (cur() == 'e' || cur() == 'E')) { bump();\n      if (cur() == '+' || cur() == '-') bump();\n      while (i < src.length() && Character.isDigit(cur())) bump(); }\n    return Double.parseDouble(src.substring(start, i));\n  }\n\n  private Token keyword(int sl, int sc) {\n    if (src.startsWith(\"true\", i))  { i += 4; col += 4; return new Token(TokType.TRUE, \"true\", 0, sl, sc); }\n    if (src.startsWith(\"false\", i)) { i += 5; col += 5; return new Token(TokType.FALSE, \"false\", 0, sl, sc); }\n    if (src.startsWith(\"null\", i))  { i += 4; col += 4; return new Token(TokType.NULL, \"null\", 0, sl, sc); }\n    throw new ParseException(\"unexpected character '\" + cur() + \"'\", sl, sc);\n  }\n}\n\nfinal class JsonParser {\n  private static final int MAX_DEPTH = 512;     // hostile-input guard\n  private final Lexer lex; private Token look; private int depth = 0;\n\n  JsonParser(String src) { lex = new Lexer(src); look = lex.next(); }\n\n  static JsonValue parse(String src) {\n    JsonParser p = new JsonParser(src);\n    JsonValue v = p.parseValue();\n    p.expect(TokType.EOF);                      // trailing garbage is an error\n    return v;\n  }\n\n  private Token expect(TokType t) {\n    if (look.type() != t)\n      throw new ParseException(\"expected \" + t + \" but found \" + look.type(), look.line(), look.col());\n    Token cur = look; look = lex.next(); return cur;\n  }\n\n  private JsonValue parseValue() {\n    if (++depth > MAX_DEPTH)\n      throw new ParseException(\"nesting deeper than \" + MAX_DEPTH, look.line(), look.col());\n    try {\n      return switch (look.type()) {\n        case LBRACE   -> parseObject();\n        case LBRACKET -> parseArray();\n        case STRING   -> new JsonString(expect(TokType.STRING).text());\n        case NUMBER   -> new JsonNumber(expect(TokType.NUMBER).num());\n        case TRUE     -> { expect(TokType.TRUE);  yield JsonBoolean.TRUE; }\n        case FALSE    -> { expect(TokType.FALSE); yield JsonBoolean.FALSE; }\n        case NULL     -> { expect(TokType.NULL);  yield JsonNull.INSTANCE; }\n        default -> throw new ParseException(\"value expected\", look.line(), look.col());\n      };\n    } finally { depth--; }\n  }\n\n  private JsonValue parseObject() {\n    expect(TokType.LBRACE);\n    Map<String, JsonValue> members = new LinkedHashMap<>();\n    if (look.type() == TokType.RBRACE) { expect(TokType.RBRACE); return new JsonObject(members); }\n    while (true) {\n      String key = expect(TokType.STRING).text();\n      expect(TokType.COLON);\n      members.put(key, parseValue());            // duplicate key: last wins (documented)\n      if (look.type() == TokType.COMMA) { expect(TokType.COMMA); continue; }\n      expect(TokType.RBRACE); return new JsonObject(members);\n    }\n  }\n\n  private JsonValue parseArray() {\n    expect(TokType.LBRACKET);\n    List<JsonValue> items = new ArrayList<>();\n    if (look.type() == TokType.RBRACKET) { expect(TokType.RBRACKET); return new JsonArray(items); }\n    while (true) {\n      items.add(parseValue());\n      if (look.type() == TokType.COMMA) { expect(TokType.COMMA); continue; }\n      expect(TokType.RBRACKET); return new JsonArray(items);\n    }\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the lexer/parser split and one-token lookahead",
        "Start from the property that makes recursive descent legal here: JSON is LL(1). At every decision point in the grammar, ONE token of lookahead uniquely selects the production. Seeing LBRACE you must be starting an object; seeing STRING inside an object you must be starting a member; there is no production where two alternatives begin with the same token, so the parser never backtracks and never needs a second token of lookahead. That property is what lets parseValue be a single switch with no speculation, and it is the first thing to say when an interviewer asks why not a parser generator: the grammar is small enough and unambiguous enough that generated tables would be strictly more machinery for strictly less readable code. Now the split itself. The lexer's job is to make the parser's world tidy: whitespace has already been skipped, a string is a decoded Java String rather than a run of bytes with escape sequences in it, and a number is already a double. Because the lexer's contract is 'give me the next token', it is a flat state machine over characters that never asks a grammatical question — you can unit-test it by feeding character sequences and asserting token streams, which is far easier than testing a merged reader. The parser's job is symmetrical: it never touches src, only look. The single lookahead field plus two helpers — peek (implicit here: reading look.type()) and expect(type) which consumes-or-throws — is the whole protocol, and getting it exactly right matters. Note carefully that expect() advances the lookahead by pulling the NEXT token before returning the current one; a beginner's version that forgets to refill look silently reparses the same token and produces an infinite loop on the first comma. Note also that parse() calls expect(EOF) after the top-level value: without it, the input 'legal JSON followed by garbage' parses successfully, which is a real vulnerability class when the same bytes are later reinterpreted by a stricter downstream parser. The other consequence of the split is that a STREAMING API is nearly free: the same Lexer feeding an event handler instead of a tree builder gives you a pull parser for documents too big for memory, and the parser methods become the tree-building policy rather than the only possible consumer."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the details that separate a toy from a real parser — escapes, numbers, depth, position",
        "FOUR details are where interviewers separate candidates who have written a parser from candidates who have read about one. STRING ESCAPES AND UNICODE: \\uXXXX yields a UTF-16 code UNIT, not a code point. Characters outside the Basic Multilingual Plane are written as a SURROGATE PAIR — a high surrogate in D800..DBFF followed by a low surrogate in DC00..DFFF — and a parser that decodes each escape into a Java char and appends it gets this right almost by accident, because Java strings are UTF-16 and the two appended chars recompose into the correct code point. A parser that decodes into a code-point int and calls appendCodePoint per escape gets it WRONG, producing two replacement characters. Strict parsers additionally validate the pairing: a lone high surrogate is malformed input, and silently emitting it produces a string that cannot be encoded back to valid UTF-8 — the bug surfaces three services downstream. Raw control characters below 0x20 inside a string are also illegal and must be rejected rather than passed through. NUMBER PRECISION: JSON numbers are arbitrary-precision decimal text; doubles are 53-bit binary. Parsing 9007199254740993 into a double silently returns 9007199254740992, and a 64-bit database ID mangled by a JSON round-trip is a genuine production incident, not a hypothetical. The honest design keeps the numeric TEXT on the token and decides lazily: long when it fits, double when it has a fraction or exponent, BigDecimal on demand — Jackson's use-BigDecimal-for-floats flag exists precisely because of this. 1e309 overflows to Infinity, which JSON cannot represent, so strict mode must reject rather than emit. DEPTH LIMITING: recursive descent recurses, so nesting depth is stack depth. A ten-thousand-bracket input from an attacker is a StackOverflowError; worse, StackOverflowError is an Error, not an Exception, so a handler catching Exception does not catch it and the thread — sometimes the whole request pipeline — dies. The fix is the explicit counter in parseValue with a configurable MAX_DEPTH, converting an unrecoverable Error into a normal ParseException. This is why every mature JSON library ships a depth limit (Jackson's StreamReadConstraints, serde_json's recursion limit); mentioning that a limit EXISTS in real libraries is the strongest possible evidence you have production scars. ERROR POSITION: track line and column in the lexer's bump() — the one place characters are consumed — and stamp them onto the token at its START. Position tracked in the parser points at wherever scanning stopped, which for an unterminated string is the end of the file rather than the quote that opened it."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "RECURSIVE DESCENT vs EXPLICIT STACK: recursion mirrors the grammar and is far easier to read, but its depth is bounded by the thread stack, hence the depth cap. An iterative parser with an explicit stack of containers moves nesting onto the heap, so the limit becomes memory rather than stack — genuinely better for untrusted input, materially harder to follow. Most libraries choose recursion plus a limit; say which you would pick and why. DOM vs STREAMING: building the full tree is convenient and is what an interviewer expects by default, but memory is O(document). A pull parser (next-token) or a push parser (SAX-style events) processes a gigabyte with constant memory at the cost of a client that must track its own context; the lexer/parser split means both APIs sit on the same lexer, which is the design's payoff — mention that the split BUYS this rather than treating streaming as a rewrite. EAGER vs LAZY VALUES: some high-performance parsers (simdjson's on-demand API) only materialize the parts of the tree you access, trading random-access convenience for enormous speed on documents where you read three fields out of two hundred. STRICT vs LENIENT: comments, trailing commas, single quotes, unquoted keys and NaN are all common real-world extensions; make them explicit opt-in flags on the Config object, never silent tolerance — a parser that accepts more than the spec becomes a compatibility trap for everyone downstream, and the asymmetry (be strict in what you emit, configurable in what you accept) deserves a sentence. DUPLICATE KEYS are unspecified by RFC 8259: last-wins is the common choice, but a security-conscious parser rejects them because two parsers disagreeing about which value wins is a documented request-smuggling technique. Extensions to expect: a Visitor for serialization and pretty-printing; JSON Pointer path lookup over the tree; SCHEMA VALIDATION as a separate pass over the composite rather than checks smuggled into parsing (parsing validates SYNTAX, schema validates MEANING, and conflating them makes both untestable); and object mapping onto POJOs, which is a different problem — reflection or codegen over an already-parsed tree — worth naming as out of scope for the parser proper."),
      C("deep_dive", "Common follow-ups & how it evolves",
        "Expect: 'Why split lexer and parser at all?' — two independent state machines (character-level escapes, grammar-level productions) become one incomprehensible method when merged; split, each is testable alone and a streaming API becomes free. 'How much lookahead do you need?' — exactly one, because the JSON grammar is LL(1); no production is ambiguous on its first token, so there is no backtracking. 'What happens on ten thousand nested arrays?' — without a depth cap, StackOverflowError, which is an Error and slips past catch(Exception); with the counter in parseValue it is an ordinary ParseException naming the limit. 'Parse 9007199254740993.' — as a double it silently becomes ...992; keep the numeric text on the token and choose long, double, or BigDecimal by shape, and reject 1e309 in strict mode rather than emitting Infinity. 'Emoji written as two \\u escapes?' — they are UTF-16 surrogate halves; append each decoded escape as a char and Java recomposes the pair, and validate that a high surrogate is followed by a low one. 'How do you report errors?' — line and column tracked in the lexer's single character-consuming method, stamped onto the token at its start, so the message points at the opening quote of an unterminated string rather than at EOF. 'Trailing garbage after the top-level value?' — an explicit expect(EOF) rejects it; omitting that check is a parser-differential vulnerability. 'Add comments and trailing commas.' — an opt-in leniency flag in the lexer only; the grammar and the parser are untouched, which is the split paying rent again. 'Make it stream.' — same lexer, a handler interface instead of tree construction; the tree builder becomes one implementation of that handler."),
    ],
    "Writing a JSON parser is the most common 'implement something real' LLD prompt at parser-adjacent and infrastructure teams, and it grades things a design conversation cannot fake. Interviewers look for the lexer/parser separation stated as a deliberate choice, recursive descent justified by the grammar being LL(1) with one token of lookahead, and then they probe the edges: surrogate pairs, number precision beyond a double, the missing expect(EOF), and above all the depth cap — because a candidate who volunteers that untrusted deeply-nested input causes a StackOverflowError, and that Error escapes catch(Exception), has demonstrably operated a parser in production. Weak answers write a character-by-character reader with a grammar and an escape state machine tangled together, then discover they cannot produce a useful error message.",
    [
      G("merged_lexer_parser", "Writing one method that walks characters while also tracking grammatical position, so escape decoding and production selection interleave.", "Split into a Lexer producing Tokens (whitespace, escapes, unicode, number scanning) and a recursive-descent Parser over that token stream with one token of lookahead; each becomes independently testable and streaming falls out for free.", "a single parse() method containing both a backslash case and a comma/colon case; index arithmetic inside grammar functions; no Token type in the design at all."),
      G("no_depth_limit", "Recursing on nested values with no depth counter, so hostile input blows the JVM stack.", "Increment a depth counter on entry to parseValue and throw a ParseException past MAX_DEPTH (512 is typical), converting an uncatchable StackOverflowError into normal error handling; mention that every mature library ships this limit.", "parseValue with no depth field; error handling that catches Exception around parsing; no mention of untrusted input in the requirements discussion."),
      G("naive_number_and_escapes", "Calling Double.parseDouble for every number and decoding \\uXXXX as a code point, losing 64-bit integer precision and breaking surrogate pairs.", "Keep the number TEXT on the token and pick long / double / BigDecimal by shape, rejecting overflow in strict mode; decode each \\u escape to a UTF-16 char so surrogate pairs recompose, and validate pairing plus reject raw control characters.", "large integer IDs changing value after a JSON round-trip; appendCodePoint on a single \\u escape; emoji becoming two replacement characters; 1e309 silently parsing to Infinity."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.jsonparser, "JSON parser class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── DEPENDENCY-INJECTION CONTAINER ───────────────────────
  TT("lld_m13", "lld_m13_t6", 6, "Design a Dependency-Injection Container", "design-di-container",
    ["case-study", "reflection", "graph", "lifecycle"],
    "OrderService needs a PaymentGateway and an InventoryClient; InventoryClient needs a HttpClient and a Config; the HttpClient must be a singleton but the OrderService must be new per request. Then someone makes AuditService depend on OrderService and OrderService depend on AuditService, and your container answers with a StackOverflowError and a stack trace two thousand frames deep. Design a Dependency-Injection Container.",
    "A container is a MAP from type to provider plus a RECURSIVE GRAPH WALK over constructors — that is genuinely all the happy path is. The design content is in the three things around it: LIFETIMES (singleton, transient, scoped) which decide whether resolution caches; a RESOLUTION STACK, the load-bearing mechanism, which turns a cycle from an incomprehensible StackOverflowError into 'circular dependency: OrderService to AuditService to OrderService'; and the reflection-versus-codegen axis, which is the real senior trade-off because it trades startup cost and runtime type-safety for developer convenience.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: REGISTER a mapping from a type (usually an interface) to a way of producing it — a concrete implementation class, a factory lambda, or a pre-built instance — and RESOLVE an instance of that type on demand with all of its own dependencies constructed and injected transitively. Injection is by CONSTRUCTOR (the default and the only form that permits final fields and guarantees a fully-initialized object); field and setter injection are optional and worth arguing against. Support LIFETIMES: SINGLETON (one instance for the container's life), TRANSIENT (a fresh instance per resolve), and SCOPED (one per logical scope, typically a request or a unit of work, disposed when the scope closes). Support multiple registrations for one interface disambiguated by a qualifier name, and support injecting a collection of all implementations (every EventHandler, for example). DISPOSE resources deterministically: closing a scope must close what it created, in reverse construction order. Error behaviour is a first-class requirement, not an afterthought: an unregistered dependency, an ambiguous registration, and a CIRCULAR dependency must each produce a message naming the full resolution PATH — 'A requires B requires C requires A' — not a stack overflow. Non-functional: resolution sits on the request path for scoped and transient objects, so a reflective constructor call per resolve is acceptable but repeated reflective INTROSPECTION is not — reflect once at registration, cache the plan. THREAD SAFETY: singletons must be created exactly once under concurrent first-resolution. And the container must be able to FAIL FAST — validating the whole graph at startup rather than discovering a missing binding at 3am on the one code path nobody exercised. Scope for the interview: an in-process container, constructor injection, no AOP proxying, no classpath scanning beyond an optional convenience."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "REGISTRATION is the unit of configuration: the service KEY (type plus optional qualifier), a PROVIDER (a function from a resolution context to an instance — implementation class, factory, or constant), and a LIFETIME. Making the provider a function rather than always a class is what lets 'bind this interface to a lambda' and 'bind this to an already-built object' be the same mechanism as 'construct this class'. REGISTRY is the immutable-after-startup map from key to Registration; freezing it after configuration removes a whole class of concurrency questions and enables the startup validation pass. CONTAINER is the facade: resolve(Class) and its recursive engine, plus the singleton cache. CONSTRUCTORPLAN is the cached reflection result for one implementation class — the chosen Constructor object and the array of parameter KEYS derived from parameter types and qualifier annotations. Computing it once at registration and reusing it per resolve is the difference between a container that is a rounding error and one that shows up in a profiler. RESOLUTIONSTACK is a per-resolution (thread-local or parameter-passed) deque of keys currently being constructed; membership means a cycle, and the deque's contents ARE the error message. SCOPE is a child container holding its own instance cache and a list of disposables; it delegates unknown keys to the parent and closes its own instances on exit. LIFETIME is a small enum, but the behaviour it selects — which cache resolution consults before constructing — is what makes it real. CIRCULARDEPENDENCYEXCEPTION and UNREGISTEREDTYPEEXCEPTION carry the path."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The core algorithm is a DEPTH-FIRST WALK of the constructor graph: to build T, look up its Registration, consult the lifetime's cache, and if a construction is needed, recursively resolve each constructor parameter and then invoke the constructor with the results. Three design decisions make that walk safe. FIRST, resolution is not a lookup but a graph traversal, so it needs traversal bookkeeping — the resolution stack — exactly the way a cycle-detecting DFS does; treating the container as merely a map is the mental model that produces the StackOverflowError. SECOND, lifetime is consulted BEFORE construction, not after: for a singleton the cache check is the first thing in resolve, which is also what makes a legal diamond (two services sharing one repository) construct the repository once rather than twice. THIRD, the constructor choice must be deterministic: exactly one public constructor, or the one annotated @Inject, and anything else is a configuration error thrown at registration — a container that guesses among overloads produces behaviour nobody can predict from reading the class. Patterns, named and justified: the container is a FACTORY of factories and, at its edges, a SERVICE LOCATOR — a pattern worth explicitly disavowing for application code, because injecting the container into a class hides that class's real dependencies and destroys testability, and the whole point of DI is that a constructor signature is an honest dependency declaration. The composition root — the single place near main() where registrations happen — is the counterpart discipline. STRATEGY appears in the provider function; COMPOSITE in the parent/child scope chain; PROXY appears only as the escape hatch for cycles, discussed below. Deliberately absent: field injection via reflection into private fields (it makes objects constructible only by the container, so unit tests need the container too), and automatic classpath scanning as the default (implicit registration means the failure mode is a missing binding discovered at runtime instead of at the composition root)."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "record Key(Class<?> type, String qualifier) {}\nenum Lifetime { SINGLETON, TRANSIENT, SCOPED }\n\nrecord Registration(Key key, Function<Ctx, Object> provider, Lifetime lifetime) {}\n\nfinal class Ctx {                              // per-resolution traversal state\n  final Container container;\n  final Deque<Key> stack = new ArrayDeque<>(); // the cycle detector\n  final Scope scope;\n  Ctx(Container c, Scope s) { container = c; scope = s; }\n}\n\nfinal class ConstructorPlan {                  // reflection done ONCE, at register()\n  final Constructor<?> ctor; final Key[] params;\n  ConstructorPlan(Class<?> impl) {\n    Constructor<?>[] all = impl.getDeclaredConstructors();\n    Constructor<?> chosen = null;\n    for (Constructor<?> c : all) if (c.isAnnotationPresent(Inject.class)) chosen = c;\n    if (chosen == null) {\n      Constructor<?>[] pub = impl.getConstructors();\n      if (pub.length != 1)\n        throw new IllegalStateException(impl + \": need exactly one public ctor or @Inject\");\n      chosen = pub[0];\n    }\n    chosen.setAccessible(true);\n    ctor = chosen;\n    Parameter[] ps = ctor.getParameters();\n    params = new Key[ps.length];\n    for (int i = 0; i < ps.length; i++) {\n      Named n = ps[i].getAnnotation(Named.class);\n      params[i] = new Key(ps[i].getType(), n == null ? null : n.value());\n    }\n  }\n  Object instantiate(Object[] args) {\n    try { return ctor.newInstance(args); }\n    catch (ReflectiveOperationException e) { throw new RuntimeException(\"construct failed\", e); }\n  }\n}\n\nfinal class Container {\n  private final Map<Key, Registration> registry = new HashMap<>();\n  private final Map<Key, Object> singletons = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n  private volatile boolean frozen = false;\n\n  <T> void register(Class<T> type, Class<? extends T> impl, Lifetime life) {\n    register(type, null, impl, life);\n  }\n  <T> void register(Class<T> type, String qualifier, Class<? extends T> impl, Lifetime life) {\n    if (frozen) throw new IllegalStateException(\"registry frozen\");\n    ConstructorPlan plan = new ConstructorPlan(impl);          // reflect now, not per-resolve\n    Key k = new Key(type, qualifier);\n    registry.put(k, new Registration(k, ctx -> {\n      Object[] args = new Object[plan.params.length];\n      for (int i = 0; i < args.length; i++)\n        args[i] = ctx.container.resolveKey(plan.params[i], ctx);   // RECURSIVE walk\n      return plan.instantiate(args);\n    }, life));\n  }\n  <T> void registerInstance(Class<T> type, T instance) {\n    registry.put(new Key(type, null),\n        new Registration(new Key(type, null), ctx -> instance, Lifetime.SINGLETON));\n  }\n\n  void freezeAndValidate() {                    // FAIL FAST at startup, not at 3am\n    frozen = true;\n    for (Key k : registry.keySet()) validate(k, new ArrayDeque<>());\n  }\n  private void validate(Key k, Deque<Key> stack) {\n    if (stack.contains(k)) throw new CircularDependencyException(path(stack, k));\n    Registration r = registry.get(k);\n    if (r == null) throw new UnregisteredTypeException(k + \" required by \" + path(stack, k));\n    stack.push(k);\n    for (Key dep : dependenciesOf(k)) validate(dep, stack);\n    stack.pop();\n  }\n\n  <T> T resolve(Class<T> type) { return resolve(type, null, null); }\n\n  @SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")\n  <T> T resolve(Class<T> type, String qualifier, Scope scope) {\n    return (T) resolveKey(new Key(type, qualifier), new Ctx(this, scope));\n  }\n\n  Object resolveKey(Key k, Ctx ctx) {\n    Registration r = registry.get(k);\n    if (r == null) throw new UnregisteredTypeException(k + \" not registered; path \" + path(ctx.stack, k));\n\n    switch (r.lifetime()) {                     // lifetime consulted BEFORE constructing\n      case SINGLETON: {\n        Object cached = singletons.get(k);\n        if (cached != null) return cached;\n        break;\n      }\n      case SCOPED: {\n        if (ctx.scope == null) throw new IllegalStateException(k + \" is scoped; no active scope\");\n        Object cached = ctx.scope.get(k);\n        if (cached != null) return cached;\n        break;\n      }\n      case TRANSIENT: break;\n    }\n\n    if (ctx.stack.contains(k))                  // THE cycle check, before recursing\n      throw new CircularDependencyException(path(ctx.stack, k));\n    ctx.stack.push(k);\n    try {\n      Object instance = r.provider().apply(ctx);          // recursion happens in here\n      if (r.lifetime() == Lifetime.SINGLETON) {\n        Object prev = singletons.putIfAbsent(k, instance); // exactly-once under races\n        return prev != null ? prev : instance;\n      }\n      if (r.lifetime() == Lifetime.SCOPED) ctx.scope.put(k, instance);\n      return instance;\n    } finally { ctx.stack.pop(); }\n  }\n\n  private static String path(Deque<Key> stack, Key k) {\n    List<Key> p = new ArrayList<>(stack); Collections.reverse(p); p.add(k);\n    return p.stream().map(Key::toString).collect(Collectors.joining(\" -> \"));\n  }\n}\n\nfinal class Scope implements AutoCloseable {\n  private final Map<Key, Object> instances = new HashMap<>();\n  private final Deque<AutoCloseable> disposables = new ArrayDeque<>();\n  Object get(Key k) { return instances.get(k); }\n  void put(Key k, Object v) {\n    instances.put(k, v);\n    if (v instanceof AutoCloseable c) disposables.push(c);   // LIFO = reverse construction\n  }\n  public void close() {\n    while (!disposables.isEmpty())\n      try { disposables.pop().close(); } catch (Exception ignored) {}\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: lifetimes, the resolution walk, and exactly-once singletons",
        "LIFETIME is where a container stops being a factory and starts being a design decision, so make the semantics precise. SINGLETON means one instance per container, cached forever; the instance and everything it captures must be thread-safe, because it will be shared across every request. TRANSIENT means construct on every resolve; note the sharp consequence that a transient injected into a singleton is effectively pinned for the singleton's lifetime — the CAPTIVE DEPENDENCY problem, and it is the most common lifetime bug in real applications: a transient (or worse, a request-scoped) database session captured by a singleton service outlives its scope and starts serving stale or closed connections. A serious container detects this at validation time by checking that no registration depends on a shorter-lived one. SCOPED means one per scope; the scope is a child cache plus a disposal list, and closing it disposes in REVERSE construction order (LIFO, hence the Deque in the code) because a later-constructed object may depend on an earlier one still being open. THE RESOLUTION WALK itself is a depth-first traversal where the recursion is hidden inside the provider lambda: resolveKey looks up the registration, checks the appropriate cache, pushes its key on the stack, and calls the provider, which recursively resolves each constructor parameter. This yields the property people expect but rarely state: a DIAMOND resolves correctly. If both OrderService and AuditService need Repository and Repository is a singleton, the first resolution caches it and the second gets the same instance; if Repository were transient, they would each get their own, which is exactly what transient means and exactly the surprise people report as a bug. EXACTLY-ONCE SINGLETON CREATION under concurrency is a genuine correctness question and interviewers do ask it. A naive if-absent-then-construct-then-put is check-then-act: two threads can both miss the cache and both construct, which for a connection pool or a metrics registry is a real defect. The lazy repair shown above is putIfAbsent — construct optimistically, publish atomically, and if you lost the race return the winner's instance and discard yours; that is correct when construction is side-effect-free but wasteful and wrong when it opens sockets. The stronger form is computeIfAbsent on a ConcurrentHashMap, which serializes construction per key — except that recursive resolution inside computeIfAbsent on the SAME map deadlocks or throws in Java, so real containers use a per-key lock or a FutureTask-style memoizing box. Naming that trap is high signal, because it is the kind of thing you only know from having hit it."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: circular dependencies — detection via the resolution stack, and the proxy escape hatch",
        "This is the mechanism the whole topic exists to test. Without detection, A requiring B requiring A recurses until the JVM's stack is exhausted, and the user gets a StackOverflowError with two thousand identical frames and no indication of WHICH types formed the cycle — a genuinely awful debugging experience, and StackOverflowError being an Error means whatever framework wraps the resolve call probably does not catch it. The detection primitive is the same one a cycle-detecting DFS uses: a set of nodes currently on the recursion path. Push the key before resolving its dependencies, pop it in a finally after construction. If a key is ALREADY on the stack when resolution reaches it, the graph has a cycle, and — this is the part that makes it useful rather than merely safe — the stack's contents from that key onward ARE the cycle, so the exception message can print 'OrderService to AuditService to OrderService'. Two subtleties worth volunteering. The path set must be per-resolution, not global: it is traversal state, so it belongs in a Ctx object passed down (as above) or a ThreadLocal, and a shared mutable field would corrupt concurrent resolutions. And 'already resolved' is not the same as 'on the stack': the diamond case revisits Repository twice legitimately, which is why the check is membership in the ACTIVE path rather than in a visited set — using a visited set instead would falsely report the diamond as a cycle, a classic off-by-one-concept error. Better still is validating the whole graph at STARTUP (freezeAndValidate above): walk every registration once and every cycle and every missing binding surfaces during boot, in CI, in front of the developer who caused it, rather than on the one request path that hits that service. Now the escape hatch. Sometimes a cycle is genuinely in the domain and refactoring it away is expensive; the standard answer is a LAZY PROXY. Inject into A not a real B but a generated proxy implementing B's interface, which resolves the real B on first METHOD CALL rather than at construction. The cycle is broken because construction no longer requires the other object to exist — only usage does, and by then both are built. Say the costs plainly: the dependency must be an interface (or the proxy needs bytecode generation over a non-final class), a resolution failure moves from startup to the first call at runtime, and stack traces gain frames. Setter injection breaks cycles the same way and with the same drawback — the object is briefly incompletely constructed, so final fields are impossible and a partially-wired object can escape. The senior framing is that a cycle is usually a MODELING smell — two classes that need each other are frequently one concept or want a third mediating one, or the dependency should be an event rather than a call — and the proxy is a pragmatic bandage you apply knowingly, not a feature you advertise."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "REFLECTION vs COMPILE-TIME CODEGEN is the central axis and the one interviewers push hardest. Reflective resolution (Spring, Guice) is flexible — bindings are decided at runtime, conditional configuration and dynamic module loading are natural — and it costs: classpath introspection at startup (Spring Boot applications spending seconds before serving a request is this cost), errors that appear at runtime rather than compile time (a missing binding is an exception, not a build failure), poor GraalVM native-image compatibility because reflection targets must be registered explicitly, and unhelpful stack traces threaded through proxy frames. Compile-time containers (Dagger, Micronaut, Koin's compiler mode) run an annotation processor that GENERATES the wiring code: the container becomes plain constructor calls, startup is nearly free, a missing or circular dependency is a COMPILE ERROR with the graph printed, and native images work because there is no reflection left. The price is build-time complexity, generated code to debug through, and genuine loss of runtime dynamism — you cannot decide a binding from a config file the processor never saw. State the decision rule rather than a favourite: Android and serverless and native images push hard toward codegen because startup and binary size dominate; long-running servers with plugin-ish dynamism and heavy framework integration tolerate reflection. The MIDDLE GROUND — reflect once at registration into a cached ConstructorPlan, as this design does — captures most of the runtime win while keeping runtime configuration, and is the honest answer for a hand-rolled container. Other trade-offs: CONSTRUCTOR vs FIELD injection (constructor gives final fields, a fully-initialized object, an honest dependency list, and testability without the container; field injection hides dependencies and makes classes uninstantiable in a plain unit test — resist it even though it is less typing); EXPLICIT REGISTRATION vs CLASSPATH SCANNING (explicit is verbose but makes the composition root the single readable truth; scanning is convenient until two implementations of an interface appear and resolution becomes ambiguous by accident); EAGER vs LAZY singleton instantiation (eager surfaces construction failures at boot and warms caches, lazy speeds startup and skips unused services — production servers usually want eager for exactly the fail-fast reason). Extensions to expect: decorators and interceptors (wrap the resolved instance — the door to AOP, logging, and transaction management, and also the door to proxy-shaped stack traces); multi-binding a collection of implementations; hierarchical containers for plugin isolation; and a health check that resolves everything at boot, which is just freezeAndValidate with instantiation turned on."),
      C("deep_dive", "Common follow-ups & how it evolves",
        "Expect: 'A depends on B depends on A — what does your container do?' — the resolution stack contains A when resolution reaches A again, so it throws CircularDependencyException printing the full path; without that stack it is a StackOverflowError nobody can debug. 'Why not just a visited set?' — a visited set would reject legal diamonds; the check must be membership in the ACTIVE resolution path, popped in a finally. 'Break the cycle without refactoring.' — inject a lazy proxy over the interface that resolves on first method call, or fall back to setter injection; both trade a startup guarantee for a runtime one, and both are a bandage over a modeling smell. 'Two threads resolve a singleton simultaneously.' — putIfAbsent publishes exactly one instance; if construction is expensive or side-effecting, use a per-key lock or memoizing future, and note that computeIfAbsent deadlocks under recursive resolution. 'Singleton depending on a request-scoped object?' — captive dependency: the scoped instance is pinned past its scope; validate that no registration depends on a shorter lifetime, or inject a provider/factory instead of the instance. 'Where does reflection happen and how often?' — once per registration, producing a cached ConstructorPlan; per-resolve is only the newInstance call. 'Two implementations of one interface?' — qualifiers on the Key, or multi-binding into a collection; ambiguity must be an error, never an arbitrary pick. 'Why is injecting the container itself bad?' — that is Service Locator: dependencies become invisible in the constructor signature, tests need the container, and compile-time checkability is gone. 'How does this become Dagger?' — move the same graph walk to an annotation processor at build time: the cycle check becomes a compile error, startup cost disappears, and you surrender runtime dynamism."),
    ],
    "The DI container is a favourite senior prompt because it is a graph problem wearing a framework costume: candidates who see it as 'a map of types' produce something that dies on a cycle, and candidates who see it as a depth-first constructor walk naturally produce the resolution stack, correct lifetime caching, and diamond handling. Interviewers reliably drive at three things — how a circular dependency is detected and REPORTED (path, not stack overflow), what singleton, transient and scoped actually mean including the captive-dependency trap, and where you land on reflection versus compile-time codegen with startup cost, native-image support and compile-time safety as the axes. Volunteering fail-fast startup validation and exactly-once singleton creation under concurrency is what separates a good answer from a framework-user's answer.",
    [
      G("no_cycle_detection", "Resolving dependencies recursively with no record of what is currently being constructed, so a cycle becomes a StackOverflowError.", "Keep a per-resolution stack of keys: push before resolving dependencies, pop in a finally, and throw a CircularDependencyException naming the full path when a key is already present; validate the entire graph at startup so cycles surface at boot.", "resolve() that only looks up a map and recurses; a StackOverflowError with repeating frames; error messages that name one type but not the path; a visited set used instead of an active-path stack."),
      G("lifetime_confusion", "Treating every registration as a singleton (or every one as transient), and injecting shorter-lived objects into longer-lived ones.", "Model lifetime explicitly and consult its cache BEFORE constructing; forbid a longer-lived registration from depending on a shorter-lived one (captive dependency), injecting a provider/factory instead when the dependency must be per-call.", "a singleton holding a request-scoped session or connection; scoped objects never disposed; construction happening before any cache lookup; stale data appearing only after the first request."),
      G("reflect_every_resolve", "Calling getConstructors and reading annotations on every resolve, and constructing singletons with check-then-put under concurrency.", "Reflect once at registration into a cached ConstructorPlan (chosen constructor + parameter keys) and publish singletons with putIfAbsent or a per-key lock so exactly one instance wins.", "getDeclaredConstructors inside resolve; reflection showing up in a profiler flame graph; two instances of a supposed singleton under load; no plan/cache object in the design."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.dicontainer, "Dependency-injection container class diagram"),
  // ─────────────────── PLUGIN SYSTEM ────────────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m13", "lld_m13_t7", 7, "Design a Plugin System", "design-plugin-system",
    ["case-study", "classloader", "lifecycle", "extensibility"],
    "Your product must let third parties add features without you shipping a release, and without their code taking the host down. Plugin A bundles Guava 21, plugin B bundles Guava 32, plugin C needs plugin A started before it, and plugin D has an infinite loop in its start() method. What loads what, in which order, and what happens when D never returns? Design a Plugin System.",
    "The whole design is one boundary drawn twice. First as a CONTRACT: a small, versioned host-owned interface that plugins implement and a HostContext that hands them exactly the capabilities they may use — extension points, not access to your internals. Second as ISOLATION: each plugin gets its own child-first CLASSLOADER so its transitive dependencies cannot collide with the host's or each other's, with only the contract package delegated to the parent. Around that boundary sit an explicit LIFECYCLE (load, init, start, stop, unload), a TOPOLOGICAL SORT for inter-plugin dependency ordering, and failure containment so one bad plugin degrades one feature instead of the process.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: DISCOVER plugins from a directory of jars (or a registry), each carrying a MANIFEST declaring an id, a version, the host API version it was built against, its dependencies on other plugins, and its entry-point class. LOAD each plugin, INSTANTIATE its entry point, and drive it through a lifecycle the host controls: init (wire it up, give it its context and configuration — no work yet), start (it may now register handlers, open resources, serve traffic), stop (release everything, cleanly), unload (drop it entirely). Plugins EXTEND the host at defined EXTENSION POINTS — contribute a menu item, a request filter, an exporter, an event handler — and may declare DEPENDENCIES on other plugins, which must therefore start in dependency order and stop in reverse. Support enable/disable and, ideally, hot install and hot update without restarting the host. Non-functional requirements are where the difficulty lives. ISOLATION: two plugins bundling incompatible versions of the same library must both work, which a single flat classpath cannot deliver. STABILITY: a plugin that throws, hangs, or leaks must not take down the host or other plugins — failure is contained at the plugin boundary and reported, and a plugin that repeatedly fails gets quarantined. COMPATIBILITY: the host will evolve, so the contract must be VERSIONED and old plugins must either keep working or be refused with a clear reason, never load and then fail with NoSuchMethodError deep inside a call. SECURITY: third-party code in your process can read your files and your memory, so state the trust model honestly — same-process plugins are trusted-ish and hardening is partial, while genuine untrusted code belongs out of process. Scope for the interview: JVM in-process plugins, jar discovery, no OSGi."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "PLUGIN is the SPI — the service provider interface the host owns and publishes: a handful of lifecycle methods (init(HostContext), start(), stop()) and nothing else. It lives in a separate, tiny api artifact that plugins compile against, and that artifact's stability IS the compatibility promise. PLUGINDESCRIPTOR is the parsed manifest: id, version, required host apiVersion range, dependency ids with version ranges, entry-point class name, and the jar location — read WITHOUT loading any plugin class, so the host can reason about the whole set before executing a line of third-party code. HOSTCONTEXT is the capability object handed to each plugin at init: the extension registry it may contribute to, a scoped logger, its own configuration slice, and a service lookup limited to interfaces the host chose to expose. It is deliberately not the host object itself — handing over 'everything' is the design failure that makes every internal refactor a plugin-breaking change. PLUGINCLASSLOADER is a URLClassLoader per plugin with an inverted (child-first) delegation policy for everything except the api package. EXTENSIONREGISTRY maps extension-point interfaces to the contributed implementations, tagged with the contributing plugin id so a stop() can revoke exactly that plugin's contributions. PLUGINMANAGER is the orchestrator: discover, validate versions, topologically order, load, drive lifecycle, contain failures, quarantine. PLUGINSTATE is the explicit enum (DISCOVERED, RESOLVED, LOADED, STARTED, STOPPED, FAILED) with guarded transitions, because 'is it running?' answered by three booleans is how plugin managers become unmaintainable."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The organising idea is that a plugin system is an INVERSION of the normal dependency direction: the host does not call plugin classes it knows about; it calls an interface it defined, implemented by code it has never seen. Everything else follows from protecting that interface. Keep the SPI SMALL — every method on it is a promise you cannot break for years — and keep the CAPABILITY OBJECT explicit, because what a plugin can reach is what you can never refactor. Patterns, justified rather than listed: STRATEGY/SPI at the contract itself; OBSERVER at extension points so the host fires events and contributed handlers receive them without the host knowing who is listening; FACADE at HostContext, whose purpose is precisely to narrow what is reachable; and STATE for the lifecycle enum, where legal transitions are encoded in one place so a double-start or a start-after-fail is rejected rather than half-executed. The lifecycle is deliberately FOUR phases rather than a constructor, and the reason is worth stating: constructing an object, wiring it, and letting it begin doing work are three different moments, and a plugin that opens a socket in its constructor cannot be validated before it acts. init separates wiring from action; start is the only point after which the plugin is live; stop must be idempotent because it runs on the shutdown path and on the failure path. The other structural decision is that the manager NEVER trusts a plugin call: every lifecycle invocation and every extension dispatch goes through a wrapper that catches Throwable, applies a timeout, and sets the plugin's context class loader — three lines that turn 'a plugin crashed the server' into 'a plugin was quarantined'. What is deliberately absent: a shared mutable host singleton reachable by plugins (that is the coupling the context object exists to prevent), and reflection-based duck typing instead of an interface (it moves every compatibility failure to runtime)."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "public interface Plugin {                       // the host-owned SPI, kept tiny\n  void init(HostContext ctx) throws Exception;\n  void start() throws Exception;\n  void stop() throws Exception;\n}\n\nrecord PluginDescriptor(String id, Version version, VersionRange hostApi,\n                        Map<String, VersionRange> dependsOn,\n                        String entryClass, Path jar) {}\n\nenum PluginState { DISCOVERED, RESOLVED, LOADED, STARTED, STOPPED, FAILED }\n\nfinal class PluginClassLoader extends URLClassLoader {\n  private static final String API_PKG = \"com.host.plugin.api.\";\n  PluginClassLoader(Path jar, ClassLoader parent) throws Exception {\n    super(new URL[]{ jar.toUri().toURL() }, parent);\n  }\n  @Override\n  protected Class<?> loadClass(String name, boolean resolve) throws ClassNotFoundException {\n    synchronized (getClassLoadingLock(name)) {\n      Class<?> c = findLoadedClass(name);\n      if (c == null) {\n        // Contract types MUST come from the parent, or host and plugin get two\n        // incompatible copies of Plugin and the cast fails with ClassCastException.\n        if (name.startsWith(API_PKG) || name.startsWith(\"java.\") || name.startsWith(\"javax.\"))\n          c = super.loadClass(name, false);              // parent-first\n        else {\n          try { c = findClass(name); }                   // CHILD-FIRST: own deps win\n          catch (ClassNotFoundException e) { c = super.loadClass(name, false); }\n        }\n      }\n      if (resolve) resolveClass(c);\n      return c;\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfinal class PluginManager {\n  private final Map<String, Loaded> plugins = new LinkedHashMap<>();\n  private final ExtensionRegistry registry = new ExtensionRegistry();\n  private final Version hostApiVersion;\n  private final ExecutorService lifecycleExec = Executors.newCachedThreadPool();\n\n  record Loaded(PluginDescriptor d, Plugin instance, PluginClassLoader cl, PluginState state) {}\n\n  void startAll(List<PluginDescriptor> found) {\n    List<PluginDescriptor> usable = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (PluginDescriptor d : found) {                    // 1. version gate, no code run\n      if (!d.hostApi().includes(hostApiVersion)) {\n        report(d, \"needs host API \" + d.hostApi() + \", host is \" + hostApiVersion);\n        continue;\n      }\n      usable.add(d);\n    }\n    for (PluginDescriptor d : topoSort(usable))           // 2. dependency order\n      loadAndStart(d);\n  }\n\n  // Kahn's algorithm; a remaining node means a dependency cycle among plugins.\n  List<PluginDescriptor> topoSort(List<PluginDescriptor> ds) {\n    Map<String, PluginDescriptor> byId = new HashMap<>();\n    Map<String, Integer> indeg = new HashMap<>();\n    Map<String, List<String>> dependents = new HashMap<>();\n    for (PluginDescriptor d : ds) { byId.put(d.id(), d); indeg.putIfAbsent(d.id(), 0); }\n    for (PluginDescriptor d : ds)\n      for (String dep : d.dependsOn().keySet()) {\n        if (!byId.containsKey(dep)) throw new IllegalStateException(d.id() + \" needs missing \" + dep);\n        indeg.merge(d.id(), 1, Integer::sum);\n        dependents.computeIfAbsent(dep, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(d.id());\n      }\n    Deque<String> ready = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    indeg.forEach((id, n) -> { if (n == 0) ready.add(id); });\n    List<PluginDescriptor> order = new ArrayList<>();\n    while (!ready.isEmpty()) {\n      String id = ready.poll(); order.add(byId.get(id));\n      for (String dep : dependents.getOrDefault(id, List.of()))\n        if (indeg.merge(dep, -1, Integer::sum) == 0) ready.add(dep);\n    }\n    if (order.size() != ds.size())\n      throw new IllegalStateException(\"plugin dependency cycle among \" + missing(byId, order));\n    return order;\n  }\n\n  private void loadAndStart(PluginDescriptor d) {\n    PluginClassLoader cl = null;\n    try {\n      cl = new PluginClassLoader(d.jar(), getClass().getClassLoader());\n      Class<?> c = cl.loadClass(d.entryClass());\n      Plugin p = (Plugin) c.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();\n      HostContext ctx = new HostContext(d.id(), registry, configFor(d), loggerFor(d));\n      guarded(d, cl, () -> p.init(ctx));\n      guarded(d, cl, p::start);\n      plugins.put(d.id(), new Loaded(d, p, cl, PluginState.STARTED));\n    } catch (Throwable t) {                                // containment, not propagation\n      report(d, \"failed to start: \" + t);\n      registry.revokeAll(d.id());\n      closeQuietly(cl);\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Every crossing into plugin code: right classloader, bounded time, catch Throwable.\n  private void guarded(PluginDescriptor d, ClassLoader cl, ThrowingRunnable body) throws Exception {\n    Future<?> f = lifecycleExec.submit(() -> {\n      ClassLoader prev = Thread.currentThread().getContextClassLoader();\n      Thread.currentThread().setContextClassLoader(cl);\n      try { body.run(); } finally { Thread.currentThread().setContextClassLoader(prev); }\n      return null;\n    });\n    try { f.get(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS); }\n    catch (TimeoutException e) { f.cancel(true); throw new IllegalStateException(d.id() + \" lifecycle timed out\"); }\n    catch (ExecutionException e) { throw new IllegalStateException(d.id() + \" threw: \" + e.getCause(), e.getCause()); }\n  }\n\n  void stopAll() {\n    List<String> ids = new ArrayList<>(plugins.keySet());\n    Collections.reverse(ids);                              // reverse dependency order\n    for (String id : ids) {\n      Loaded l = plugins.get(id);\n      try { guarded(l.d(), l.cl(), l.instance()::stop); } catch (Exception ignored) {}\n      registry.revokeAll(id);\n      closeQuietly(l.cl());                                // then drop every reference\n    }\n    plugins.clear();\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the contract boundary, versioning, and classloader isolation (and how it leaks)",
        "THE CONTRACT is the part you can never take back, so design it like an API you must support for five years. Two rules do most of the work. Keep it MINIMAL — three lifecycle methods plus the extension-point interfaces, published as a separate api jar that plugins compile against and that has no dependencies of its own. And keep it EXPLICITLY VERSIONED with semantic-versioning semantics the host enforces at LOAD time from the manifest, before any plugin class is loaded: a plugin declaring api range [2.0, 3.0) is refused by a 3.x host with a message naming the mismatch. The alternative — load it and find out — produces NoSuchMethodError or AbstractMethodError halfway through a request, from a class the operator has never heard of. Adding a method to a published SPI is a breaking change unless it has a default implementation, which is why default methods on interfaces are the standard evolution tactic; removing or re-signaturing anything is a major bump. ISOLATION is the second half. On a single flat classpath, whichever jar the loader reaches first wins, so plugin A bundling Guava 21 and plugin B bundling Guava 32 means one of them silently runs against the wrong version — the failure appears as a NoSuchMethodError in a library neither author touched. The fix is one CLASSLOADER PER PLUGIN with INVERTED delegation: standard Java delegation is parent-first (ask the parent, then look locally), which would hand both plugins the host's Guava; child-first flips it so the plugin's own jar wins for its own dependencies. The critical exception, and the thing interviewers check, is that the API PACKAGE MUST STAY PARENT-FIRST. Class identity in the JVM is (name, defining classloader), so if a plugin's loader defines its own copy of the Plugin interface, the host's cast to Plugin throws ClassCastException with the maddening message that com.host.plugin.api.Plugin cannot be cast to com.host.plugin.api.Plugin. That single exception line is a rite of passage; being able to explain it is strong signal. Now name where isolation LEAKS, because a candidate who claims classloaders give real isolation has not run one. Static state in classes loaded by the PARENT is shared by everyone (a plugin calling System.setProperty or mutating a host singleton affects all). Threads a plugin starts inherit its context classloader and outlive its lifecycle unless you kill them. ThreadLocals set by a plugin on a host thread pool thread pin the plugin's classes forever. JDBC drivers, shutdown hooks, and JMX registrations register into JVM-global tables. And nothing in the classloader model prevents plugin code from reading files, opening sockets, or calling System.exit — that is a SECURITY boundary, and the JVM no longer has one worth relying on (the SecurityManager is deprecated for removal), so genuinely untrusted code goes out of process, into a separate JVM or a WASM/container sandbox with IPC."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: lifecycle, dependency ordering, unloading leaks, and failure containment",
        "LIFECYCLE ORDERING is a graph problem: plugin C depending on A and B must start AFTER both and stop BEFORE either, so the manager topologically sorts the dependency DAG (Kahn's algorithm above), starts in that order, and stops in exactly reverse. A leftover node after Kahn's queue drains means a CYCLE among plugins, which — unlike a DI cycle, where a lazy proxy is available — is best refused outright with the cycle printed, because two plugins each expecting the other to be started first have no correct order. Version RANGES on dependencies make this resolution rather than mere ordering: if C needs A in [1.2, 2.0) and the installed A is 2.1, C is refused with a reason at resolve time. UNLOADING is the hard part and the honest answer is 'harder than it looks'. To truly unload a plugin, every class it defined must be garbage-collectible, which requires its ClassLoader to be unreachable, which requires that NOTHING holds a reference to any of its classes or instances: no listener still registered in the host's extension registry, no callback in a host collection, no running thread whose class belongs to it, no ThreadLocal on a pooled thread, no cached Class object in a serialization framework, no JDBC driver in DriverManager's static list. Miss one and the classloader is pinned — every reload of that plugin adds another full copy of its classes to metaspace, and the process dies of java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace after a dozen hot deploys. This is the CLASSLOADER LEAK, and it is the reason mature systems either restart to update plugins or invest heavily in revocation discipline: the registry tags every contribution with its plugin id (revokeAll(id) in the code), the manager tracks threads and executors it handed out, and stop() is required to be complete. Saying 'and I would verify unload with a heap dump checking the loader is collected' is exactly the sort of concrete production detail that lands. FAILURE CONTAINMENT is the third mechanism. Every crossing into plugin code is wrapped: catch THROWABLE, not Exception, because a plugin's StackOverflowError or LinkageError would otherwise unwind through the host; apply a TIMEOUT by running lifecycle calls on an executor and cancelling on expiry, which handles the plugin whose start() never returns; set the thread context classloader for the duration, because libraries the plugin calls will use it to load resources; and, on failure, mark the plugin FAILED, revoke its contributions, close its loader, and keep the host running with one feature missing. Add a circuit-breaker-style quarantine for plugins that fail repeatedly at an extension point, so a broken event handler stops being called rather than throwing on every event. The honest caveat to state: a timeout plus Future.cancel only interrupts cooperative code — a tight loop ignoring interruption cannot be stopped in-process at all, and Thread.stop is unsafe and removed. That limitation is precisely the argument for out-of-process plugins when the host must be hard-real-time about its own liveness."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "IN-PROCESS vs OUT-OF-PROCESS is the top-level decision and it should be argued from the trust model, not from convenience. In-process (classloaders, as designed here) gives direct method calls with no serialization, shared memory, and microsecond latency, at the price of partial isolation, no true resource limits, and no way to stop uncooperative code. Out-of-process — a separate JVM or a subprocess speaking a defined protocol, the model HashiCorp's go-plugin and Language Server Protocol chose — gives real isolation: a crashed plugin is a dead child process the host restarts, CPU and memory are capped by the OS, and a hostile plugin cannot read host memory. It costs IPC latency, serialization of everything crossing the boundary, and a much heavier development experience. The modern middle is a WASM sandbox: real memory and syscall isolation, in-process speed, but a restricted language and host-function surface. Say which you would pick for which trust level — first-party extensions in-process, marketplace plugins out-of-process or WASM. CHILD-FIRST vs PARENT-FIRST delegation: child-first gives dependency independence but risks two copies of a type that crosses the boundary, so the api package must be excluded and shared types must be kept to the contract; parent-first is simpler and fine when all plugins are built against one dependency set (an internal-only plugin system can legitimately choose it and skip the whole classloader chapter — knowing when NOT to build isolation is judgment). OSGi vs HAND-ROLLED: OSGi solves versioned isolation, dynamic install/uninstall, and service registries thoroughly and has been doing it for two decades; it also brings a large conceptual surface, its own manifest headers, and famously painful diagnostics — most teams should either adopt it wholesale or build the fifty-line version above, and the failure mode is building an accidental half-OSGi over three years. STATIC vs DYNAMIC LOADING: requiring a restart to install a plugin removes the entire unloading problem for free, which for many products is the correct trade — hot deploy is a feature with a metaspace-leak bill attached. Extensions to expect: a marketplace with signature verification (verify the jar signature before you load a line of it, since after loading it is too late), per-plugin resource accounting via a dedicated thread pool per plugin so one plugin's slowness does not exhaust a shared pool, a permissions manifest declaring what the plugin will access so users can consent, and configuration/UI contribution points."),
      C("deep_dive", "Common follow-ups & how it evolves",
        "Expect: 'Two plugins need different versions of the same library.' — one child-first ClassLoader per plugin so each resolves its own jar first, with the api package and java.* delegated to the parent. 'ClassCastException saying Plugin cannot be cast to Plugin?' — class identity is (name, defining loader); the plugin loader defined its own copy of the contract, so the api package must be parent-first. 'Plugin C depends on A and B.' — topologically sort the descriptor DAG (Kahn), start in that order, stop in reverse; a leftover node is a plugin cycle and is refused with the cycle printed. 'A plugin declares it needs host API 2.x and you are 3.0.' — refuse at RESOLVE time from the manifest, before loading any class, with a message naming both versions; loading first turns it into a NoSuchMethodError mid-request. 'A plugin start() never returns.' — lifecycle calls run on an executor with a timeout and cancel; and be honest that cancel only interrupts cooperative code, which is the strongest argument for out-of-process isolation. 'Why catch Throwable and not Exception?' — LinkageError and StackOverflowError from plugin code would otherwise unwind into the host; containment means the plugin is marked FAILED and its contributions revoked while the host keeps serving. 'Why does memory grow after every hot reload?' — a classloader leak: one surviving reference (a registered listener, a live thread, a ThreadLocal, a JDBC driver) pins the loader so its classes never unload; fix with tagged revocation on stop and verify with a heap dump. 'Can a plugin be malicious?' — in-process, effectively yes; the SecurityManager is gone, so the answer for untrusted code is process or WASM isolation plus signature verification before load. 'How does the SPI evolve?' — additive changes with default methods, versioned api artifact, and a documented deprecation window; anything else is a major bump enforced at resolve."),
    ],
    "Plugin systems are how interviewers test whether a candidate can design an API BOUNDARY rather than a class hierarchy, and the questions are unusually concrete: what exactly is in the contract, how is it versioned, what happens when two plugins bundle conflicting dependencies, in what order do dependent plugins start, and what happens when one hangs or throws. The classloader discussion is the discriminator — child-first delegation with the api package excluded, and the ClassCastException that proves you have hit the trap — followed closely by the unload/metaspace-leak question, which almost nobody volunteers unless they have operated a hot-reloading system. Strong answers also state the trust model out loud and know when to push untrusted plugins out of process entirely.",
    [
      G("no_isolation", "Loading every plugin from the host's classpath (or one shared classloader), so plugin dependencies collide with each other and with the host.", "Give each plugin its own child-first URLClassLoader that resolves its jar before the parent, delegating only the contract api package plus java.*/javax.* to the parent so shared types keep one identity.", "NoSuchMethodError in a library neither the host nor the plugin author changed; all plugin jars appended to the launch classpath; a single URLClassLoader shared across plugins."),
      G("no_lifecycle_or_containment", "Instantiating a plugin and calling it directly, letting exceptions, hangs, and errors propagate into the host, with no explicit state machine.", "Define load/init/start/stop/unload with a guarded state enum, and wrap every crossing into plugin code with catch(Throwable), a timeout on an executor, and the plugin's context classloader; on failure mark FAILED and revoke its contributions.", "plugin work started in a constructor; try/catch(Exception) around plugin calls; one plugin exception surfacing as a 500 for unrelated requests; booleans like isStarted/isBroken instead of a state enum."),
      G("unload_leak_and_unordered_start", "Assuming a plugin is gone once stop() returns, and starting plugins in directory order regardless of declared dependencies.", "Topologically sort the dependency DAG (start in order, stop in reverse, refuse cycles), and on stop revoke every tagged contribution, shut down threads/executors handed to the plugin, and drop all references so the ClassLoader becomes collectible; verify with a heap dump.", "OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace after repeated hot reloads; listeners still firing from a stopped plugin; plugins started with File.listFiles ordering; no revokeAll(pluginId) anywhere."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.plugin, "Plugin system class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── CONFIGURATION MANAGER ────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m13", "lld_m13_t8", 8, "Design a Configuration Manager", "design-config-manager",
    ["case-study", "immutability", "observer", "concurrency"],
    "The same service must read a timeout from a defaults table in dev, a YAML file in staging, an environment variable in the container, a CLI flag in a one-off debug run, and a remote feature-flag service in production — with the later ones winning. It must also pick up a change without a restart, and while it is doing that, ten thousand in-flight requests must not observe a config that is half old and half new. Design a Configuration Manager.",
    "Two mechanisms carry this design. First, LAYERED SOURCES merged in a PINNED PRECEDENCE ORDER — defaults, then file, then environment, then CLI, then remote override — resolved once into a single flat map, so 'where did this value come from' has one answer and one debuggable trace. Second, an IMMUTABLE SNAPSHOT published through a single VOLATILE REFERENCE: hot reload builds an entirely new snapshot off to the side, validates it, and swaps the reference in one write. Readers never lock and never see a half-updated state, because the state is never updated — it is replaced. Per-key mutation of a shared map is the design that produces the torn-config bug.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: read configuration from multiple SOURCES — hard-coded defaults, one or more files (YAML, properties, JSON), environment variables, command-line arguments, and optionally a remote store or feature-flag service — and expose a single lookup API. PRECEDENCE must be explicit and total: a value present in several sources resolves to exactly one winner by a documented order, and the manager must be able to say which source won for any key (an ORIGIN trace, invaluable at 2am). TYPED ACCESS: callers ask for an int, a Duration, a URL, an enum, or a list — not a String they parse at every call site. VALIDATION happens at LOAD, against a declared schema: required keys present, types parseable, ranges and enums honoured, so a typo fails the boot rather than the tenth request. HOT RELOAD: on file change, a periodic poll, or a push from the remote source, the manager picks up new values without a restart, and interested components are NOTIFIED of what changed. SECRETS: values marked secret must never appear in logs, in a toString, in an error message, or in a config dump endpoint. Non-functional requirements decide the shape. READ PATH IS HOT — get() is called from request handlers, so it must be lock-free and allocation-free; a synchronized map lookup on every read is a scalability bug in a component that should be invisible. ATOMICITY: a reload must be all-or-nothing from a reader's perspective; a request that reads two related keys must not get one from before the reload and one from after. FAIL-SAFE RELOAD: a bad new config must NOT replace a good running one — validate the candidate first, and on failure keep serving the old snapshot and alarm. Scope: in-process library, sources abstracted behind an interface, no distributed consensus."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "CONFIGSOURCE is the interface every layer implements: a name, a precedence rank, and load() returning a flat Map from key to raw String (flattening nested YAML into dotted keys — server.http.port — so all sources share one key space, which is what makes merging possible at all). Concrete sources: DefaultsSource, FileSource, EnvSource (with the standard name mapping, SERVER_HTTP_PORT to server.http.port), CliSource, RemoteSource. CONFIGSNAPSHOT is the heart of the design: an IMMUTABLE object holding the merged key-value map, a per-key ORIGIN map recording which source won, a monotonically increasing VERSION, and a load timestamp. It has no setters; every reload produces a new one. SCHEMA is the declared shape — for each key, its type, whether it is required, a default, a validator (range, regex, enum), and a SECRET flag; it drives both load-time validation and redaction. CONFIGMANAGER is the facade holding a single VOLATILE reference to the current snapshot, the typed get methods, the reload routine, and the listener list. TYPED ACCESSORS convert on read from the snapshot's string map, or — better — the snapshot stores already-converted values so the hot path is a map lookup with a cast rather than a parse. CHANGELISTENER is the Observer contract: onChange(ConfigChange) where ChangeEvent carries the changed keys with old and new values, already redacted for secrets. WATCHER is whatever triggers a reload — a file-system watch service, a poll timer, or a push subscription — and it is deliberately a separate collaborator so the reload logic is testable without touching a disk."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The design is best described as a small pipeline plus a publication primitive. The PIPELINE runs on load: ask each ConfigSource for its map, merge them in ascending precedence so later sources overwrite earlier ones while recording the winning origin per key, then validate the merged result against the Schema, then convert every value to its declared type, then build the immutable snapshot. Every hard question — precedence, provenance, validation, typing — is answered once, at load, in one place, rather than repeatedly and inconsistently at call sites. The PUBLICATION primitive is the volatile reference swap, discussed at length below, and it is what makes the read path free. Patterns, chosen with reasons: OBSERVER for change notification, because components that cache derived state (a thread pool sized from config, a compiled regex, an HTTP client with a timeout) must be told to rebuild, and polling get() on every use is both slower and wrong for objects that cannot change in place. STRATEGY for ConfigSource so a new layer is a new class registered with a precedence rank — this is the extension point that gets exercised most often in real life. IMMUTABLE VALUE OBJECT for the snapshot, which is the single most important structural choice. BUILDER for constructing a snapshot during reload. What is deliberately ABSENT is as instructive: no singleton with static mutable state (it makes tests order-dependent and unmockable — inject the manager instead), no per-key setters on the public API (config is INPUT to the process, and a runtime setter turns it into shared mutable state whose provenance is untraceable), and no lock around get(). One more design point worth volunteering: bind typed, validated config into small immutable CONFIG OBJECTS (a ServerConfig record with port, host, and timeout) rather than passing the manager around, so components declare exactly what they need and can be unit-tested with a plain record — the same honest-dependency argument as constructor injection."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "interface ConfigSource {\n  String name();\n  int precedence();                       // higher wins\n  Map<String, String> load() throws Exception;\n}\n\nfinal class ConfigSnapshot {              // IMMUTABLE: never mutated, only replaced\n  private final Map<String, Object> values;    // already type-converted\n  private final Map<String, String> origin;    // key -> winning source name\n  final long version; final Instant loadedAt;\n\n  ConfigSnapshot(Map<String, Object> v, Map<String, String> o, long version) {\n    this.values = Map.copyOf(v);               // defensive: truly unmodifiable\n    this.origin = Map.copyOf(o);\n    this.version = version;\n    this.loadedAt = Instant.now();\n  }\n  @SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")\n  <T> T get(String key, Class<T> type) {\n    Object v = values.get(key);\n    if (v == null) throw new MissingConfigException(key);\n    return (T) v;                              // hot path: lookup + cast, no parse\n  }\n  String originOf(String key) { return origin.get(key); }\n  Set<String> keys() { return values.keySet(); }\n  Object raw(String key) { return values.get(key); }\n}\n\nfinal class ConfigManager {\n  private final List<ConfigSource> sources;    // sorted ascending by precedence\n  private final Schema schema;\n  private final List<ChangeListener> listeners = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();\n  private final AtomicLong versionSeq = new AtomicLong();\n\n  // THE publication primitive: one volatile reference, swapped whole.\n  private volatile ConfigSnapshot current;\n\n  ConfigManager(List<ConfigSource> sources, Schema schema) {\n    this.sources = sources.stream()\n        .sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(ConfigSource::precedence)).toList();\n    this.schema = schema;\n    this.current = build();                    // failure here fails the boot: fail fast\n  }\n\n  // Reads are lock-free: one volatile read, one map lookup.\n  int getInt(String key)          { return current.get(key, Integer.class); }\n  String getString(String key)    { return current.get(key, String.class); }\n  Duration getDuration(String k)  { return current.get(k, Duration.class); }\n  ConfigSnapshot snapshot()       { return current; }   // pin for multi-key consistency\n\n  private ConfigSnapshot build() {\n    Map<String, String> merged = new LinkedHashMap<>();\n    Map<String, String> origin = new LinkedHashMap<>();\n    for (ConfigSource s : sources) {           // ascending precedence: later overwrites\n      Map<String, String> m;\n      try { m = s.load(); }\n      catch (Exception e) { throw new ConfigLoadException(s.name() + \" failed: \" + e, e); }\n      for (var en : m.entrySet()) {\n        merged.put(en.getKey(), en.getValue());\n        origin.put(en.getKey(), s.name());      // provenance recorded as we go\n      }\n    }\n    Map<String, Object> typed = schema.validateAndConvert(merged);  // throws on bad config\n    return new ConfigSnapshot(typed, origin, versionSeq.incrementAndGet());\n  }\n\n  /** Hot reload: build fully, validate fully, THEN swap. Never mutate in place. */\n  void reload() {\n    ConfigSnapshot candidate;\n    try {\n      candidate = build();                     // if this throws, old config keeps serving\n    } catch (RuntimeException e) {\n      log.error(\"config reload REJECTED, keeping v{}: {}\", current.version, e.getMessage());\n      metrics.increment(\"config.reload.failed\");\n      return;                                  // fail-safe: bad config never goes live\n    }\n    ConfigSnapshot old = current;\n    List<Change> diff = diff(old, candidate);\n    if (diff.isEmpty()) return;                // no-op reloads must not fire listeners\n    current = candidate;                       // ← the atomic swap: one volatile write\n    notifyListeners(new ConfigChange(old.version, candidate.version, diff));\n  }\n\n  private List<Change> diff(ConfigSnapshot a, ConfigSnapshot b) {\n    Set<String> all = new TreeSet<>(a.keys()); all.addAll(b.keys());\n    List<Change> out = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (String k : all) {\n      Object oldV = a.raw(k), newV = b.raw(k);\n      if (Objects.equals(oldV, newV)) continue;\n      boolean secret = schema.isSecret(k);      // never leak a secret into an event/log\n      out.add(new Change(k, secret ? \"***\" : String.valueOf(oldV),\n                            secret ? \"***\" : String.valueOf(newV)));\n    }\n    return out;\n  }\n\n  private void notifyListeners(ConfigChange ev) {\n    for (ChangeListener l : listeners)          // one bad listener must not block the rest\n      try { l.onChange(ev); }\n      catch (Throwable t) { log.error(\"config listener {} failed\", l, t); }\n  }\n\n  void addListener(ChangeListener l) { listeners.add(l); }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: layered precedence, provenance, and validating at load rather than at read",
        "PRECEDENCE is the first thing to pin down because every configuration bug report starts with 'but I set it in the file'. The conventional chain — hard-coded defaults, then packaged config file, then environment-specific file, then environment variables, then command-line arguments, then remote overrides — is ordered by SPECIFICITY and by how urgently a human needs to override: defaults are the least specific and lose to everything; a CLI flag is a deliberate act by an operator on this one run, so it beats the file; a remote kill-switch beats even that because it must work when nobody can restart the process. Rank each source with an integer and merge in ascending order so later writes win; the merge is a single loop, and its simplicity is the point — precedence exists in ONE place rather than as scattered if-null-then-check-elsewhere logic at call sites, which is how the same key ends up resolving differently in two parts of the same program. Record PROVENANCE while merging: for each key, the name of the source that last wrote it. This costs one extra map and repays it the first time someone asks why the timeout is 30 seconds when the file clearly says 5 (answer: an environment variable set by the deployment template three layers up). Expose it through a redacted config-dump endpoint and half of all config incidents become a thirty-second lookup. Two merging subtleties worth naming: nested structures must be FLATTENED to dotted keys before merging, because merging trees raises the question of whether a nested object replaces or merges (both answers surprise someone; flat keys make it unambiguous), and LISTS have the same problem — replace-whole-list is the predictable rule and 'append across layers' is the rule that produces mysteries. VALIDATION AT LOAD, NOT AT READ is the second discipline. The naive design stores strings and calls Integer.parseInt inside getInt, which means a typo in a rarely-read key is a NumberFormatException on the one request that reads it, at 3am, from a call site with no context. Validating the whole merged map against a declared schema at load — required keys present, every value parseable to its declared type, ranges and enums honoured, cross-key invariants such as min < max checked — moves that failure to startup, where it belongs, and lets the snapshot store ALREADY-CONVERTED values so the read path is a lookup and a cast rather than a parse and an allocation. The same schema declares which keys are SECRET, which is what makes redaction systematic rather than a grep for the word password: mark at the schema, and the dump endpoint, the change events, and toString all honour it in one place. Complete the picture by never logging the raw merged map, keeping secret values in a type that refuses to serialize itself, and preferring a reference to a secret manager over the secret's value in any file."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: hot reload without torn reads — immutable snapshot + volatile swap",
        "This is the mechanism the topic is really about, and the naive version is seductive. It looks like this: keep a ConcurrentHashMap, and on reload iterate the new values calling map.put for each changed key. Every individual put is thread-safe, the map is concurrent, and it seems obviously fine. It is not. Consider a request handler that reads db.host and db.port to open a connection, while a reload changes both because the database moved. The handler reads the OLD host, the reload writes both keys, the handler reads the NEW port — and connects to the old host on the new port. This is a TORN READ, and no amount of per-key thread-safety prevents it, because the atomicity required is across the whole configuration, not per entry. The same defect appears with a max that becomes smaller than a min for a few microseconds, or a feature flag and its associated parameters disagreeing. Thread-safe containers give you per-operation atomicity; the invariant here is a multi-key one, and it needs a different primitive. That primitive is IMMUTABLE SNAPSHOT PLUS ATOMIC REFERENCE SWAP. Build the entire new configuration off to the side into a fresh ConfigSnapshot whose maps are unmodifiable copies. Validate it completely. Then perform ONE write: current = candidate, where current is a volatile field (or an AtomicReference). Every reader does a single volatile READ of that field and then works with the object it got, which can never change under it because nothing ever mutates a snapshot. A reader that grabbed the old snapshot finishes its request entirely on the old config — consistent, if slightly stale — and the next reader gets the new one. Consistency comes from the object being frozen, not from coordination, which is why the read path needs no lock at all. Two details make the argument rigorous rather than hand-wavy. VOLATILE IS LOAD-BEARING for VISIBILITY and SAFE PUBLICATION: without it, the Java Memory Model permits a reader thread to see a stale reference indefinitely, and — worse — to see the new reference while seeing the snapshot's internal fields as not-yet-initialized, because the constructor's writes could be reordered past the reference publication. The volatile write establishes a happens-before edge with every subsequent volatile read, so a thread that sees the new reference is guaranteed to see the fully-constructed object. Final fields inside the snapshot give a similar guarantee, and using both is the belt-and-braces answer interviewers like. MULTI-KEY CONSISTENCY WITHIN ONE OPERATION requires the caller to PIN the snapshot: calling manager.getInt twice is two independent volatile reads that can straddle a swap, so a handler needing several related keys should call snapshot() once and read everything from that object. Exposing that method — and explaining why — is the detail that shows you understood the mechanism rather than memorised the pattern. Finally, FAIL-SAFE RELOAD falls out for free: validation happens on the candidate before the swap, so a bad config file simply never becomes current, and the process keeps serving the last good version while alarming. Notification comes after the swap, computed as a DIFF between the two snapshots so listeners are told exactly what changed; fire listeners off the reload thread with each one wrapped in a try/catch so one slow or broken listener neither blocks the reload nor prevents its peers from being told."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "SNAPSHOT SWAP vs PER-KEY MUTATION: the swap costs a full rebuild and a transient second copy of the config in memory (measured in kilobytes — irrelevant), and buys lock-free reads and whole-config atomicity. Per-key mutation avoids the copy and buys torn reads. There is no real contest at configuration sizes, and the honest statement of when copy-on-write loses — very large state mutated very frequently — is what shows you are applying a principle rather than a reflex. PUSH vs POLL for reload: filesystem watch (WatchService, inotify) is instant but notoriously fiddly across platforms, fires multiple events for one editor save (hence debouncing), and misses changes when a file is replaced by rename rather than written in place; polling every N seconds is dumb, portable, and bounded-latency, which is why many production systems poll despite having a watcher available. Remote sources push, which is instant and also means your config plane is now a dependency with an availability budget — cache the last good remote values on disk so a remote outage does not take the service down on restart. HOT-RELOADABLE vs RESTART-ONLY KEYS: some values genuinely cannot change at runtime (a listening port already bound, a thread pool's core size in some pools, a database URL with an open pool), so mark keys in the schema as reloadable or restart-only and REJECT or ignore changes to the latter with a clear log line — silently accepting a change that has no effect is worse than refusing it, because the operator believes it worked. TYPED CONFIG OBJECTS vs STRING KEYS: binding into records (ServerConfig, RetryConfig) gives compile-time safety, autocompletion, and unit tests without a manager, but complicates hot reload because a component holding a record does not see updates — the standard resolution is to hand out a Supplier or to re-bind on the change event, and knowing that wrinkle is the sign of having done it. SECRETS: keeping them in the same snapshot is convenient but means they sit in heap where a dump exposes them; the stronger design fetches secrets from a dedicated store with short-lived leases and keeps only references in config, which trades simplicity for a genuine reduction in blast radius. Extensions to expect: per-tenant or per-environment overlays (another source layer, precedence unchanged); feature flags with targeting rules (a remote source whose values are functions of a user context — the point where a config manager becomes a flag system and the snapshot must include the rules); config versioning and rollback (keep the last N snapshots and swap back — trivial precisely because they are immutable); and an audit log of who changed what, which the origin map already half provides."),
      C("deep_dive", "Common follow-ups & how it evolves",
        "Expect: 'A file value and an environment variable disagree — which wins and how do you know?' — an integer precedence rank per source and a single ascending merge; the winning source is recorded per key and exposed through a redacted dump, so provenance is a lookup rather than an investigation. 'Reload changes two related keys — can a request see one old and one new?' — not if the snapshot is immutable and published by a single volatile write; the request either has the old object or the new one. If it calls get twice it can still straddle the swap, so it should pin one snapshot per operation. 'Why volatile and not just a normal field?' — visibility and safe publication: without it readers may never see the new reference, and may see the reference before the object's fields are initialized because the constructor's writes can be reordered past the publication. 'Why not a ConcurrentHashMap with per-key puts?' — per-key atomicity is the wrong granularity; the invariant spans keys, so putting them one at a time is a torn read by construction. 'The new config file is invalid.' — validate the candidate fully before the swap; on failure log, increment a metric, alarm, and keep serving the last good snapshot — a bad config never goes live. 'How do components react to a change?' — Observer with a diff of changed keys, fired after the swap, each listener wrapped so one failure does not block the others; components holding derived objects (pools, clients) rebuild in the callback. 'A key that cannot be changed at runtime?' — mark it restart-only in the schema and refuse the change loudly rather than pretending it applied. 'How do you keep a password out of the logs?' — the schema marks it secret, and redaction is applied centrally in the dump, the change events, and toString; better still, store a reference and fetch from a secret manager. 'How does this scale to a fleet?' — a remote source with push plus a local cached fallback; the object design is unchanged, which is the point — the snapshot swap is already the right concurrency model for a pushed update."),
    ],
    "Configuration looks like the least interesting thing in the codebase, which is exactly why it makes a discriminating interview question: everyone has used one, few have thought about the concurrency. The prompt reliably converges on two probes. First, precedence and validation — can you state a total ordering of sources, record provenance, and move type errors from the read path to boot? Second, and decisively, hot reload: candidates who reach for a ConcurrentHashMap and per-key puts get walked into the torn-read scenario, while candidates who answer 'immutable snapshot, build fully, validate, then one volatile reference swap' have demonstrated the copy-on-write publication idiom that recurs everywhere from routing tables to service registries. The follow-ups about why volatile matters for safe publication, and about pinning a snapshot for multi-key reads, separate memorised pattern from understood mechanism.",
    [
      G("per_key_mutation", "Hot-reloading by putting changed keys into a shared ConcurrentHashMap one at a time, believing per-key thread safety is enough.", "Build a complete new immutable snapshot, validate it, then publish with ONE volatile reference swap; readers take a snapshot and see a wholly consistent config, and a bad candidate never becomes current.", "config.put(key, value) inside a reload loop; readers occasionally combining old and new related values; a lock around get(); no version or snapshot object in the design."),
      G("parse_at_read", "Storing raw strings and parsing/validating inside getInt/getDuration, so a bad or missing value fails on the request that happens to read it.", "Validate the whole merged map against a declared schema at LOAD — required keys, types, ranges, cross-key invariants — and store already-converted values so the read path is a lookup and a cast.", "Integer.parseInt inside a getter; NumberFormatException in a request handler; a config typo discovered days after deploy; no schema object anywhere."),
      G("implicit_precedence_and_secrets", "Letting precedence emerge from the order sources happen to be consulted, with no record of which source won, and logging the merged config including passwords.", "Give each source an explicit precedence rank, merge ascending in one place, record the winning source per key for a provenance dump, and mark secret keys in the schema so redaction applies centrally to dumps, change events, and toString.", "if (envValue != null) else if (fileValue != null) ladders at call sites; the same key resolving differently in two components; passwords visible in startup logs or an actuator-style config endpoint."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.config, "Configuration manager class diagram"),
  // ─────────────────── URL SHORTENER (OOD) ──────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m14", "lld_m14_t1", 1, "Design a URL Shortener (OOD)", "design-url-shortener-ood",
    ["case-study", "strategy", "encoding", "concurrency"],
    "Turn a 200-character URL into a seven-character code, resolve it back in one lookup, let a marketing team claim the custom alias /summer-sale before a competitor does, expire links after 30 days, and count clicks — all as an OBJECT design, not a distributed system. What generates the code, and what makes two simultaneous requests for the same alias resolve to exactly one owner?",
    "The one mechanism that carries this design is CODE GENERATION, and there are exactly three candidate strategies with different failure modes: base62 of a monotonic counter (no collisions ever, but codes are sequential and enumerable), random-with-collision-retry (unguessable, but you must state the birthday math and the retry loop), and hash-then-probe (deterministic dedup, but truncation collisions need probing). Make it a STRATEGY interface and the rest of the object model — ShortLink, LinkStore, expiry, analytics — falls out cleanly. The second mechanism is uniqueness under concurrency: a custom alias is claimed by ONE atomic insert-if-absent, never by a check-then-insert.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: shorten(longUrl) returns a short CODE; resolve(code) returns the original URL or reports that it is unknown or expired. Support CUSTOM ALIASES chosen by the user, which must be unique and must be reserved against a blocklist (reserved paths like api or admin, and profanity). Support EXPIRY — an optional time-to-live after which resolution fails and the code may be recycled or tombstoned — and OWNERSHIP, so a user can list, edit the target of, and delete their own links. Record ANALYTICS per resolution: a click count, and enough detail (timestamp, referrer, coarse geography, device class) for a stats view. Optionally deduplicate: the same long URL from the same owner may reuse an existing code. Validate the input URL — scheme is http or https, host is resolvable-looking, and the target is not the shortener itself (a redirect loop) nor a known-malicious domain. Non-functional requirements that shape the OBJECT design specifically: codes must be SHORT (six to eight characters) and URL-SAFE, which fixes the alphabet at base62 or base64url; resolution is the hot path and must be a single primary-key lookup with no scanning; code assignment must be UNIQUE under concurrent requests, which is a real correctness requirement and the sharpest one here; and analytics must not slow resolution, so counting is asynchronous. State the scoping decision explicitly and confidently, because it is what the prompt is testing: this is an object-oriented design question, so the answer is classes, responsibilities, and the generation strategy — sharding, caching layers, geo-replication, and CDN redirects are the system-design version of the same prompt and belong in one closing paragraph, not in the middle of the class model."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "SHORTLINK is the aggregate: code (the identity), longUrl, ownerId, createdAt, expiresAt (nullable), an active flag, and a reference to its stats. It is deliberately mostly immutable — code and createdAt never change — and exposes behaviour rather than raw fields: isExpired(clock), a target resolution method, and validation on construction so an invalid ShortLink cannot exist. CODEGENERATOR is the Strategy interface: nextCode(longUrl) returning a candidate code. Its implementations are the design's real content — Base62CounterGenerator wrapping a monotonic id source, RandomCodeGenerator drawing from a secure random over the alphabet, and HashCodeGenerator taking a truncated digest of the URL. BASE62CODEC is a tiny pure utility (encode a long to base62, decode back) kept separate because it is trivially unit-testable and reused. LINKSTORE is the persistence port with a deliberately narrow interface, and one method on it is load-bearing: putIfAbsent(code, link) returning false when the code is taken — the atomic primitive that makes uniqueness real. It also offers get(code), findByOwner, and delete. URLVALIDATOR and RESERVEDWORDS guard alias creation. CLICKSTATS holds counters and recent events, mutated off the resolution path — an atomic counter plus an event queue drained by a background aggregator. URLSHORTENER is the service facade: shorten, shortenWithAlias, resolve, expire, and stats, sequencing validation, generation, storage, and analytics. EXPIRYPOLICY is worth its own small object — default TTL, maximum TTL, and what happens after expiry (tombstone versus recycle), because that choice has consequences discussed below."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The organising decision is to make code generation a STRATEGY rather than an if-statement, because the three approaches have genuinely different properties — collision behaviour, guessability, orderedness, and dedup — and a real product changes its mind (public links want unguessable codes, internal short links are happy with sequential ones). With the interface in place, shorten() reads the same regardless: generate a candidate, attempt an atomic insert, and on rejection either fail (custom alias taken) or retry (random strategy). That single flow is the design's spine, and it deliberately puts the uniqueness guarantee in the STORE rather than in the generator, because only the store can make a decision atomic. A second decision: resolution is a pure lookup returning either a target or an explicit outcome — NOT_FOUND, EXPIRED, DISABLED — modelled as a sealed result type rather than null, so the caller must handle each case and the HTTP layer can map them to distinct statuses (301 or 302, 404, 410 Gone for an expired link, which is a nice detail). A third: analytics is fired through an OBSERVER (or a queue) so that resolve() does one read and returns; incrementing a shared counter synchronously on every redirect makes the hottest path in the system contend on one cache line, and that is the wrong place to spend. Patterns beyond Strategy and Observer: BUILDER for ShortLink creation with its several optional fields; REPOSITORY/PORT for LinkStore so the in-memory test double and the database implementation are interchangeable, which matters because the atomicity requirement must be satisfiable by both (a ConcurrentHashMap.putIfAbsent and a unique-index insert have the same contract, which is a satisfying thing to point out). What is deliberately absent: a shared mutable counter object handed around, and any design where the service checks existence and then writes — the two-step shape is the bug this topic exists to expose."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "final class Base62 {\n  private static final char[] A =\n    \"0123456789ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz\".toCharArray();\n  static char[] alphabet() { return A.clone(); }\n  static String encode(long n) {\n    if (n == 0) return \"0\";\n    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n    while (n > 0) { sb.append(A[(int) (n % 62)]); n /= 62; }\n    return sb.reverse().toString();\n  }\n}\n\ninterface CodeGenerator { String nextCode(String longUrl); }\n\n/** Sequential: cannot collide, but codes are guessable and enumerable. */\nfinal class Base62CounterGenerator implements CodeGenerator {\n  private final AtomicLong seq;                       // or a DB sequence / id block\n  Base62CounterGenerator(long start) { seq = new AtomicLong(start); }\n  public String nextCode(String longUrl) { return Base62.encode(seq.incrementAndGet()); }\n}\n\n/** Random: unguessable; collisions handled by the store's atomic insert + retry. */\nfinal class RandomCodeGenerator implements CodeGenerator {\n  private static final char[] A = Base62.alphabet();\n  private final SecureRandom rnd = new SecureRandom();\n  private final int len;\n  RandomCodeGenerator(int len) { this.len = len; }\n  public String nextCode(String longUrl) {\n    char[] out = new char[len];\n    for (int i = 0; i < len; i++) out[i] = A[rnd.nextInt(A.length)];\n    return new String(out);\n  }\n}\n\nfinal class ShortLink {\n  final String code; final String longUrl; final String ownerId;\n  final Instant createdAt; final Instant expiresAt;   // null = never\n  private volatile boolean active = true;\n  private final LongAdder clicks = new LongAdder();   // contention-friendly counter\n\n  ShortLink(String code, String longUrl, String ownerId, Instant expiresAt) {\n    this.code = code; this.longUrl = longUrl; this.ownerId = ownerId;\n    this.createdAt = Instant.now(); this.expiresAt = expiresAt;\n  }\n  boolean isExpired(Clock clock) { return expiresAt != null && Instant.now(clock).isAfter(expiresAt); }\n  void disable() { active = false; }\n  boolean isActive() { return active; }\n  void recordClick() { clicks.increment(); }\n  long clickCount() { return clicks.sum(); }\n}\n\ninterface LinkStore {\n  /** ATOMIC: true iff this call created the entry. The uniqueness primitive. */\n  boolean putIfAbsent(String code, ShortLink link);\n  Optional<ShortLink> get(String code);\n  void remove(String code);\n}\n\nsealed interface Resolution permits Resolution.Found, Resolution.NotFound,\n                                    Resolution.Expired, Resolution.Disabled {\n  record Found(String longUrl) implements Resolution {}\n  record NotFound() implements Resolution {}\n  record Expired() implements Resolution {}\n  record Disabled() implements Resolution {}\n}\n\nfinal class UrlShortener {\n  private static final int MAX_ATTEMPTS = 5;\n  private final CodeGenerator generator;\n  private final LinkStore store;\n  private final ReservedWords reserved;\n  private final ClickCollector analytics;             // async, off the hot path\n  private final Clock clock;\n\n  String shorten(String longUrl, String ownerId, Duration ttl) {\n    UrlValidator.requireValid(longUrl);\n    Instant exp = ttl == null ? null : Instant.now(clock).plus(ttl);\n    for (int attempt = 0; attempt < MAX_ATTEMPTS; attempt++) {\n      String code = generator.nextCode(longUrl);\n      ShortLink link = new ShortLink(code, longUrl, ownerId, exp);\n      if (store.putIfAbsent(code, link)) return code;  // atomic claim wins or loses\n      // lost the race (random strategy collision): try a fresh code\n    }\n    throw new CodeSpaceExhaustedException(\"no free code after \" + MAX_ATTEMPTS + \" attempts\");\n  }\n\n  /** Custom alias: exactly ONE insert decides the owner. No check-then-insert. */\n  String shortenWithAlias(String longUrl, String alias, String ownerId, Duration ttl) {\n    UrlValidator.requireValid(longUrl);\n    if (!alias.matches(\"[A-Za-z0-9_-]{3,32}\")) throw new InvalidAliasException(alias);\n    if (reserved.contains(alias))               throw new ReservedAliasException(alias);\n    Instant exp = ttl == null ? null : Instant.now(clock).plus(ttl);\n    ShortLink link = new ShortLink(alias, longUrl, ownerId, exp);\n    if (!store.putIfAbsent(alias, link)) throw new AliasTakenException(alias);\n    return alias;                               // the insert IS the uniqueness check\n  }\n\n  Resolution resolve(String code) {\n    Optional<ShortLink> found = store.get(code);\n    if (found.isEmpty())                 return new Resolution.NotFound();\n    ShortLink l = found.get();\n    if (!l.isActive())                   return new Resolution.Disabled();\n    if (l.isExpired(clock))              return new Resolution.Expired();   // 410 Gone\n    analytics.record(code, Instant.now(clock));   // queued; resolve() does not block\n    return new Resolution.Found(l.longUrl);\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: code generation — counter vs random vs hash, with the collision math",
        "COUNTER PLUS BASE62 is the strategy with the cleanest correctness story: take a monotonically increasing id and encode it in a 62-character alphabet, and collisions are IMPOSSIBLE by construction because distinct ids encode to distinct strings — there is no retry loop, no probing, and no probability to reason about. The capacity is easy to state and you should state it: 62^6 is about 56.8 billion codes and 62^7 about 3.5 trillion, so seven characters comfortably outlives most products. Its costs are equally concrete. Codes are SEQUENTIAL, so anyone can enumerate every link ever created by incrementing — a genuine privacy failure for a service that shortens unlisted documents, and the reason this strategy is unacceptable for anything user-private. Codes also LEAK VOLUME: a competitor creates one link a day and reads your creation rate off the code. And the counter itself is shared mutable state; in one process an AtomicLong is fine, and the moment there are two processes it must become a database sequence or, better, pre-allocated BLOCKS of ids handed out to each instance (instance A takes 1..10000, B takes 10001..20000) so the coordination happens once per ten thousand links instead of once per link. Common mitigations for enumerability — encoding a scrambled id, or a bijective transform of the counter — preserve uniqueness while hiding the order, and are worth naming as the middle path. RANDOM PLUS RETRY generates n characters from a secure random and relies on the store's atomic insert to reject a duplicate. Here you must produce the birthday math rather than hand-wave, because that is exactly what is being probed. With a 62-character alphabet and length 7 the space is 3.5 trillion; the expected number of collisions when inserting k codes is approximately k squared over 2N, so at 100 million links that is 10^16 over 7 times 10^12, roughly 1,400 expected collisions total — a rate low enough that a retry loop essentially never runs twice, and high enough that having NO collision handling would be a real bug. The practical rule to state: random needs the atomic insert plus a bounded retry (five attempts), and the fill ratio must stay far below the space size — when the space is more than a few percent full, retries climb and the answer is to lengthen the code, not to raise the retry count. Use SecureRandom rather than Random, since a predictable PRNG makes codes guessable and defeats the whole point of choosing random. HASH PLUS PROBE takes a digest of the long URL (MD5 or SHA-256), truncates it to the code length, and inserts. Its virtue is DEDUPLICATION: the same URL naturally maps to the same code, so repeated shortening is idempotent for free. Its costs are that truncation reintroduces collisions — two different URLs can share a prefix of their digests, so you must detect the collision on insert and PROBE (append a salt or a counter and rehash) — and that dedup is often wrong at the product level, because two users shortening the same URL usually want separate links with separate ownership and separate analytics. The senior conclusion, and the right answer to 'which would you pick': random-with-retry for public user-facing links because unguessability is a product requirement; counter-with-blocks for internal or high-volume systems where enumerability is acceptable and zero-collision simplicity is worth more; hash only when idempotent dedup is genuinely desired. Making it a Strategy means the decision is one line of wiring rather than a rewrite."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: uniqueness under concurrency, expiry, and keeping analytics off the hot path",
        "CUSTOM ALIAS UNIQUENESS is the concurrency question hiding in a prompt that looks like it has none. The natural code is: if (store.get(alias).isEmpty()) store.put(alias, link); else throw. Two marketing teams request /summer-sale within the same millisecond; both calls to get return empty; both put; the second silently overwrites the first, and a team that received a success response now owns a link pointing somewhere else. This is check-then-act again — the same TOCTOU shape as a seat reservation or a coupon budget — and the repair is identical: ONE atomic operation whose success IS the answer. In-process that is ConcurrentHashMap.putIfAbsent; in a relational store it is an INSERT against a PRIMARY KEY or UNIQUE index, catching the duplicate-key violation as the taken case; in Mongo it is an insert on the unique _id or an upsert with a not-exists filter. Note precisely why the unique index is the correct home for this rule: it is the only place where the constraint is enforced regardless of how many application instances exist or which code path writes, and a candidate who says 'the database enforces uniqueness, the application just interprets the failure' has understood where invariants belong. The same primitive covers generated codes: the random strategy's retry loop is exactly 'putIfAbsent failed, so someone else has that code, so draw again'. EXPIRY has two design questions worth answering rather than glossing. First, expiry is evaluated at READ time (isExpired against a clock) rather than by a sweeper deleting rows, because read-time evaluation is always correct even if the sweeper is late or down; a background job then reclaims storage lazily. Return a distinct EXPIRED outcome, not NOT_FOUND, so the HTTP layer can answer 410 Gone — the semantically right status, and the kind of detail that reads as care. Second, whether an expired code may be REUSED is a policy with security consequences: recycling a code means an old QR code or an old email link silently starts pointing at a stranger's destination, which is a real phishing vector, so the safe default is TOMBSTONE — never reissue a code that has ever been live — and only recycle in systems where links are known-ephemeral. Injecting a Clock rather than calling Instant.now() directly also makes expiry unit-testable, a small thing interviewers notice. ANALYTICS is the third mechanism. Resolution is the hottest path in the system, so it must not do a synchronous write. Incrementing a plain long is not thread-safe; incrementing an AtomicLong is correct but serialises every redirect on one cache line under contention, which is measurable at high rates; LongAdder spreads the count across cells and sums on read, which is exactly the trade you want for write-heavy counters read rarely. Detailed events (timestamp, referrer, geography) go onto a bounded queue drained by a background aggregator, so a slow analytics store causes dropped or delayed metrics rather than slow redirects — and BOUNDED is the load-bearing word, because an unbounded queue in front of a slow consumer is an out-of-memory error waiting for a traffic spike. Say what you sacrifice: click counts become eventually consistent and may lose a few events on a crash, which for analytics is the right trade and for billing would not be."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "STRATEGY CHOICE, restated as a decision table because interviewers like a crisp answer: counter-base62 gives zero collisions, trivial capacity math, and enumerable codes — good internally, wrong for private links; random gives unguessability at the cost of a retry loop and a fill-ratio constraint — the default for public products; hash gives free dedup at the cost of probe-on-collision and product-level wrongness when two users want distinct links to the same target. CODE LENGTH is a real trade: shorter is friendlier on a billboard and exhausts sooner; the honest method is to pick length from the projected link count and the acceptable fill ratio rather than from aesthetics, and to note that lengthening later is easy (new codes get longer, old ones keep working) while shortening is impossible. TOMBSTONE vs RECYCLE on expiry trades storage for safety, and safety should win by default. DEDUP ON or OFF changes the ownership model — with dedup, who owns the shared code and whose analytics are these? — which is why most products dedup only within one owner, if at all. SYNCHRONOUS vs ASYNCHRONOUS analytics trades exactness for latency; bounded queues make the failure mode data loss instead of an outage. STORE INTERFACE: the narrow port with putIfAbsent keeps an in-memory map and a database interchangeable, which is what lets the whole class model be unit-tested without infrastructure — and it is the seam where this LLD hands off to system design. Say that handoff explicitly and briefly, since it proves you know the boundary you were asked to respect: at scale, resolution becomes a cache-first read (the hit rate is extreme because link popularity is heavily skewed, so an LRU in front of the store absorbs most traffic), the store shards by code, the counter strategy needs id blocks per instance to avoid a global sequence, expiry sweeping becomes a TTL index, and analytics becomes a stream into a columnar store. None of that changes the class design above, which is the strongest possible argument that the object model was right. Extensions to expect: link editing (mutable target with an audit trail — and note that editing a live code is another phishing surface, so restrict it to the owner and log it), QR generation, UTM parameter handling, one-time links (resolve atomically flips active, which is a compare-and-set, not a read-then-write), password-protected links, and per-owner rate limiting on creation to stop the code space being farmed."),
      C("deep_dive", "Common follow-ups & how it evolves",
        "Expect: 'How do you generate the code?' — name all three strategies with their failure modes and pick by product requirement: random-with-retry for public links, counter-base62 for internal, hash only for deliberate dedup; put it behind a Strategy interface so the choice is wiring. 'Random codes — what about collisions?' — expected collisions are about k squared over 2N, so 100 million links in a 3.5 trillion space is roughly 1,400 over the service's lifetime; handle them with the store's atomic insert plus a bounded retry, and lengthen the code rather than raise the retry count as the space fills. 'Two users request the same custom alias simultaneously.' — never get-then-put; one atomic putIfAbsent (unique index / duplicate-key violation) decides the owner and the loser gets AliasTaken. 'Why not just check first?' — check-then-act is TOCTOU; both callers see empty and the second overwrites the first, handing a success response to someone whose link now points elsewhere. 'Can codes be enumerated?' — with a counter, yes, trivially; that is the strategy's real cost, mitigated by scrambling the id or by choosing random. 'What happens after expiry?' — evaluated at read time against an injected Clock, returned as a distinct EXPIRED outcome mapping to 410 Gone, with tombstoning by default because recycling a code silently repoints old QR codes and emails. 'Counting clicks slows the redirect?' — LongAdder for the counter and a bounded queue plus background aggregation for detailed events; accept eventual consistency and bounded loss on crash. 'Two application instances with the counter strategy?' — a global sequence is a bottleneck, so hand each instance a pre-allocated block of ids. 'Where does this become system design?' — an LRU cache in front of a sharded store, TTL indexes for expiry, and a stream for analytics; the classes above are unchanged, which is the point."),
    ],
    "URL shortener is the canonical warm-up that quietly grades depth: everyone can draw the two methods, so interviewers spend the time on the generation strategy and the concurrency. The discriminating moments are whether you can compare counter-base62, random-with-retry, and hash-with-probe on collision behaviour, guessability, and dedup — with the birthday approximation stated numerically rather than gestured at — and whether custom-alias uniqueness comes out as one atomic insert-if-absent instead of a check followed by a write. Secondary probes reward object-design maturity: a distinct EXPIRED result mapping to 410 rather than a null, an injected Clock, tombstoning instead of recycling codes, and analytics pushed off the redirect path with a contention-friendly counter. Candidates who drift into sharding and CDNs have answered the wrong prompt, and saying where that boundary lies earns the point instead.",
    [
      G("check_then_insert_alias", "Checking whether a custom alias exists and then inserting it as two steps, so simultaneous requests both succeed and one silently overwrites the other.", "Make the insert itself the uniqueness check: putIfAbsent in memory, a PRIMARY KEY/UNIQUE index insert in a database with the duplicate-key violation interpreted as taken; the invariant belongs in the store, not in application logic.", "if (!exists(alias)) save(alias, link); no unique constraint on the code column; a user reporting their alias now points at someone else's URL; the same shape reused for generated codes."),
      G("collision_handwave", "Choosing random codes without stating the collision probability or writing any retry path — or choosing a sequential counter without acknowledging that codes become enumerable.", "State the math (expected collisions about k squared over 2N; 100M links in 62^7 is roughly 1,400) and pair random generation with atomic insert plus bounded retry; if using a counter, acknowledge enumerability and either scramble the id or accept it only for internal links.", "no retry loop around code assignment; a claim that collisions are impossible with random generation; java.util.Random instead of SecureRandom; no capacity calculation for the chosen code length."),
      G("hot_path_pollution", "Doing synchronous analytics writes and returning null (or NOT_FOUND) for expired links, with expiry decided by a delete job rather than at read time.", "Return a sealed Resolution with distinct NotFound/Expired/Disabled cases (410 Gone for expired), evaluate expiry at read time against an injected Clock, tombstone rather than recycle codes, and push click events onto a bounded queue with a LongAdder counter.", "resolve() writing to an analytics table before redirecting; AtomicLong incremented on every redirect under heavy contention; expired links returning 404; Instant.now() called directly so expiry cannot be unit-tested."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.shortener, "URL shortener class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── CIRCUIT BREAKER ──────────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m14", "lld_m14_t2", 2, "Design a Circuit Breaker", "design-circuit-breaker-lld",
    ["case-study", "state-machine", "resilience", "concurrency"],
    "A downstream payment provider starts timing out at three seconds. Your service keeps calling it, every request thread parks waiting, the pool exhausts, and now your checkout is down because someone ELSE broke. You want to stop calling for a while, then carefully test whether it recovered — but if a thousand threads all 'carefully test' at once you have just re-created the stampede you were escaping. Design a Circuit Breaker.",
    "Three mechanisms carry this. The STATE MACHINE — CLOSED, OPEN, HALF_OPEN — must be exact about which transitions exist and what triggers each. The FAILURE DETECTOR must be a SLIDING WINDOW failure RATE gated on a minimum call volume, not a consecutive-failure counter: one failure out of three calls must not trip a breaker, and fifty percent of two hundred calls must. And HALF_OPEN admission is a concurrency problem in disguise — exactly ONE trial request may pass, which requires a compare-and-set on the state, because a boolean flag read by two threads is read as free by both.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: wrap a call to a remote dependency so that repeated failures cause the breaker to STOP calling it — failing fast instead — and so that it automatically probes for recovery after a cooldown. Three states are required: CLOSED (calls pass through, outcomes are recorded), OPEN (calls are rejected immediately with a CallNotPermitted error or a fallback, and a timer runs), HALF_OPEN (a limited number of trial calls are permitted; success closes the breaker, failure re-opens it). Configuration must include the failure-rate threshold, the sliding-window size and type, a MINIMUM number of calls before any rate is trusted, the open-state duration, and the number of permitted half-open probes. Outcomes counted as failures must be configurable: exceptions from a declared set, timeouts, and SLOW CALLS above a latency threshold (slow is a failure — a dependency answering in nine seconds is destroying you as effectively as one that errors). The breaker must expose STATE and METRICS for dashboards and emit transition events. A FALLBACK path — cached value, default, degraded feature, or fast failure — must be expressible by the caller. Non-functional: the breaker sits on every call, so its bookkeeping must be O(1) and lock-free or nearly so; state must be correct under high concurrency, since the interesting moments (tripping, probing) happen precisely when many threads are in flight; and it must be PER DEPENDENCY, not global, so a failing recommendation service cannot open the breaker guarding the payment service. Scope for the interview: an in-process breaker for one JVM, wrapping synchronous calls, with the multi-instance question addressed at the end."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "CIRCUITBREAKER is the facade with one primary method — execute(Supplier<T>) or decorate(Supplier<T>) — and it owns the state, the window, and the transition logic. STATE is the enum CLOSED, OPEN, HALF_OPEN, held in an AtomicReference (or an atomic state object) precisely because transitions must be compare-and-set rather than plain writes. SLIDINGWINDOW is the failure detector: a fixed-size ring buffer of recent outcomes (COUNT-based) or a set of per-second buckets (TIME-based), exposing failureRate() and totalCalls() in O(1) by maintaining running counters as entries are added and evicted rather than by scanning. OUTCOME is a small value: SUCCESS, FAILURE, or SLOW, plus a duration — modelling slow calls as their own outcome type is what lets the same window drive both a failure-rate and a slow-call-rate threshold. CONFIG holds the thresholds, durations, and the exception predicate that decides whether a given Throwable counts as a failure (a business exception such as InsufficientFunds is NOT a dependency failure and must not trip the breaker — one of the most common real-world misconfigurations). CLOCK is injected so state transitions and windows are testable without sleeping, which matters more here than almost anywhere else. PERMITCOUNTER governs half-open admission, and in the simplest correct form it is not a separate object at all but the compare-and-set on the state itself. BREAKERREGISTRY maps a dependency key to its breaker so isolation per dependency is structural. EVENTPUBLISHER emits transitions for observability, off the calling path. FALLBACK is supplied by the caller, not owned by the breaker — the breaker's job is to decide permission, not to invent a degraded answer."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The breaker is a DECORATOR around a call: same signature in, same signature out, with permission checking before and outcome recording after. Expressing it that way — decorate(Supplier) returning a Supplier — is what lets it compose with a retry, a bulkhead, and a timeout, and the ORDER of that composition is a question interviewers like: the breaker should be OUTSIDE the retry, because retrying inside an open breaker means each logical call still consumes N retries against a dead dependency, while breaker-outside means the whole retried operation is rejected in one cheap failure. The timeout goes innermost, since a call with no timeout can never be recorded as slow or failed in bounded time — and a breaker in front of an untimed call is decoration over an unbounded hang. Patterns: STATE for the machine itself, and here a full State class hierarchy is genuinely defensible rather than over-engineering, because each state has different permission logic and different transition triggers; a compact alternative is an enum plus a switch in one method, and either is fine so long as the transitions are centralized rather than smeared across execute(). OBSERVER for transition events (a breaker opening is a page-worthy signal). STRATEGY at two points — the exception predicate deciding what counts as a failure, and the window implementation (count-based versus time-based). REGISTRY/FLYWEIGHT for per-dependency breakers keyed by name. The critical structural discipline is that ALL state mutation flows through two methods — onSuccess and onFailure — plus one permission check, so there is exactly one place where transitions can be reasoned about and tested. The most common bad design smears if-state-equals-OPEN checks throughout the call path, at which point no one can enumerate the reachable transitions. Also deliberately absent: no background thread flipping OPEN to HALF_OPEN on a timer. Doing it lazily — the next arriving call notices the cooldown has elapsed and attempts the CAS itself — removes a thread per breaker and, more importantly, means the transition happens exactly when there is traffic to probe with."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "enum State { CLOSED, OPEN, HALF_OPEN }\nenum Outcome { SUCCESS, FAILURE, SLOW }\n\n/** Count-based ring of recent outcomes; O(1) add with running counters. */\nfinal class SlidingWindow {\n  private final Outcome[] ring; private int idx = 0, size = 0;\n  private int failures = 0, slow = 0;\n  SlidingWindow(int capacity) { ring = new Outcome[capacity]; }\n\n  synchronized void record(Outcome o) {          // small critical section, O(1)\n    Outcome evicted = ring[idx];\n    if (evicted == Outcome.FAILURE) failures--;\n    else if (evicted == Outcome.SLOW) slow--;\n    else if (evicted != null) { /* SUCCESS: nothing to decrement */ }\n    ring[idx] = o;\n    if (o == Outcome.FAILURE) failures++;\n    else if (o == Outcome.SLOW) slow++;\n    idx = (idx + 1) % ring.length;\n    if (size < ring.length) size++;\n  }\n  synchronized int total() { return size; }\n  synchronized double failureRate() { return size == 0 ? 0.0 : (double) (failures + slow) / size; }\n  synchronized void reset() { Arrays.fill(ring, null); idx = 0; size = 0; failures = 0; slow = 0; }\n}\n\nrecord BreakerConfig(double failureRateThreshold,   // e.g. 0.5\n                     int minimumCalls,              // e.g. 20 — the volume gate\n                     int windowSize,                // e.g. 100\n                     Duration openDuration,         // e.g. 30s cooldown\n                     Duration slowCallThreshold,    // e.g. 2s counts as failure\n                     Predicate<Throwable> countsAsFailure) {}\n\nfinal class CircuitBreaker {\n  private final String name; private final BreakerConfig cfg; private final Clock clock;\n  private final SlidingWindow window;\n  private final AtomicReference<State> state = new AtomicReference<>(State.CLOSED);\n  private final AtomicLong openedAtMillis = new AtomicLong(0);\n\n  <T> T execute(Supplier<T> call, Supplier<T> fallback) {\n    if (!acquirePermission()) {                  // fail fast, no thread parked downstream\n      events.publish(name, \"call_rejected\");\n      if (fallback != null) return fallback.get();\n      throw new CallNotPermittedException(name);\n    }\n    long start = clock.millis();\n    try {\n      T result = call.get();\n      recordOutcome(clock.millis() - start >= cfg.slowCallThreshold().toMillis()\n                      ? Outcome.SLOW : Outcome.SUCCESS);\n      return result;\n    } catch (Throwable t) {\n      if (cfg.countsAsFailure().test(t)) recordOutcome(Outcome.FAILURE);\n      else                               recordOutcome(Outcome.SUCCESS);  // business error\n      throw t;\n    }\n  }\n\n  /** THE admission control. HALF_OPEN admits exactly one probe, decided by CAS. */\n  private boolean acquirePermission() {\n    State s = state.get();\n    switch (s) {\n      case CLOSED:\n        return true;\n      case OPEN:\n        if (clock.millis() - openedAtMillis.get() < cfg.openDuration().toMillis())\n          return false;                          // still cooling down\n        // Cooldown elapsed: try to become the ONE prober. Losers stay rejected.\n        return state.compareAndSet(State.OPEN, State.HALF_OPEN);\n      case HALF_OPEN:\n        return false;                            // a probe is already in flight\n      default:\n        return false;\n    }\n  }\n\n  private void recordOutcome(Outcome o) {\n    State s = state.get();\n    if (s == State.HALF_OPEN) {                  // the probe's verdict decides everything\n      if (o == Outcome.SUCCESS) transitionTo(State.HALF_OPEN, State.CLOSED);\n      else                      open(State.HALF_OPEN);\n      return;\n    }\n    window.record(o);\n    if (s == State.CLOSED\n        && window.total() >= cfg.minimumCalls()             // volume gate FIRST\n        && window.failureRate() >= cfg.failureRateThreshold())\n      open(State.CLOSED);\n  }\n\n  private void open(State from) {\n    if (state.compareAndSet(from, State.OPEN)) { // only the winner stamps the timer\n      openedAtMillis.set(clock.millis());\n      window.reset();                            // stale outcomes must not decide the future\n      events.publish(name, \"opened\");\n    }\n  }\n\n  private void transitionTo(State from, State to) {\n    if (state.compareAndSet(from, to)) {\n      if (to == State.CLOSED) window.reset();\n      events.publish(name, \"transition \" + from + \" -> \" + to);\n    }\n  }\n\n  State currentState() { return state.get(); }\n}\n\n/** Per-dependency isolation: one breaker per downstream, never one global breaker. */\nfinal class BreakerRegistry {\n  private final Map<String, CircuitBreaker> byKey = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n  CircuitBreaker forDependency(String key, BreakerConfig cfg) {\n    return byKey.computeIfAbsent(key, k -> new CircuitBreaker(k, cfg, Clock.systemUTC()));\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the three-state machine and the failure-detection window",
        "STATE THE MACHINE EXACTLY, because vagueness here is the most common way this question is failed. CLOSED is the normal state: every call passes through and every outcome is recorded into the window. The only exit is CLOSED to OPEN, triggered when the recorded failure rate crosses the threshold AND the window holds at least the minimum number of calls. OPEN rejects every call immediately — no downstream call, no thread parked — and holds a timestamp of when it opened. Its only exit is OPEN to HALF_OPEN, triggered lazily when a call arrives after the cooldown has elapsed. HALF_OPEN permits a strictly limited number of trial calls (one, in the simplest correct design) and has two exits: HALF_OPEN to CLOSED when the probe succeeds, and HALF_OPEN to OPEN when it fails, which restarts the cooldown. Two edges people invent that must NOT exist: there is no CLOSED to HALF_OPEN (half-open is only ever reached from open), and there is no automatic OPEN to CLOSED without a successful probe — closing on a timer alone means you release the full stampede against a dependency you have not tested. Also state what happens to the window on each transition: it is RESET when the breaker opens and when it closes, because outcomes recorded before an outage ended must not be counted against a dependency that has since recovered — a breaker that keeps stale failures in the window flaps immediately after closing. NOW THE DETECTOR, which is where the real design content is. The naive version counts CONSECUTIVE failures: after five failures in a row, open. It is seductively simple and wrong in two directions at once. It MISSES a dependency failing fifty percent of the time — every other call succeeds, the consecutive counter never reaches five, and the breaker never trips while half your traffic errors. And it FALSELY TRIPS on a low-volume path: a service called three times an hour that fails three times in a row opens a breaker on a sample size that means nothing, then rejects the next hour of legitimate traffic. The correct detector is a failure RATE over a SLIDING WINDOW of recent outcomes, gated by a MINIMUM CALL COUNT. The rate captures partial failure, which is the common real mode — degradation is far more frequent than a clean crash. The minimum-volume gate is the part candidates forget and interviewers specifically ask about: one failure out of one call is a hundred percent failure rate, and without a minimum-calls threshold the first error after a quiet period opens the circuit. Requiring, say, at least twenty calls in the window before the rate is trusted makes the statistic meaningful. Implementation matters too: the window must be O(1) per record, which means a ring buffer with running counters updated on insert and eviction (as in the code) rather than a list that gets scanned on every call — a detector that walks a hundred entries per request is a tax on every call in the system. Choose COUNT-based windows (last N calls) for steady traffic, and TIME-based bucketed windows (the last M seconds, in per-second buckets) when traffic is bursty and 'the last 100 calls' might span an hour of stale history. Finally, count SLOW CALLS as failures: a dependency answering successfully in eight seconds is exhausting your thread pool exactly like an error would, and the slow-call threshold is what turns a latency problem into a breaker trip instead of an outage."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: HALF_OPEN admission control, CAS, and what to do when the breaker trips",
        "HALF_OPEN IS A CONCURRENCY PROBLEM, and it is the single sharpest probe in this topic. The intent is simple — after the cooldown, let ONE request through to see if the dependency recovered — and the naive implementation is a boolean: if (!probeInFlight) { probeInFlight = true; allow(); }. Under load, a thousand threads reach the cooldown boundary within microseconds of each other; they all read probeInFlight as false; they all set it to true; they all proceed. The breaker has just released the full stampede against a dependency that may still be dead, which is precisely the failure the breaker exists to prevent, and the dependency's recovery — often a service coming back with cold caches and empty connection pools — is killed on arrival. This is check-then-act one more time, and the repair is the same primitive used for seats and coupons and aliases: a COMPARE-AND-SET whose success grants the permission. In the code above, acquirePermission for the OPEN state calls state.compareAndSet(OPEN, HALF_OPEN), and the return value IS the answer: exactly one thread's CAS succeeds and becomes the prober, every other thread sees the CAS fail (the state is no longer OPEN) and is rejected. No lock, no flag, no window in which two threads can both believe they are the probe. If policy wants N concurrent probes rather than one, the same idea scales with an AtomicInteger permit counter decremented by CAS, but the state transition itself must still be atomic. State the reason the boolean fails in memory-model terms if pushed: a non-atomic read-modify-write on a shared field has no exclusion at all, and even marking it volatile only fixes visibility, not atomicity — volatile makes the read fresh, and two threads can still both read false. THE PROBE'S VERDICT then drives the machine: success transitions HALF_OPEN to CLOSED and resets the window; failure transitions back to OPEN and restarts the cooldown, usually with EXPONENTIAL BACKOFF on repeated failed probes so a long outage is probed every thirty seconds, then a minute, then two, rather than every thirty seconds forever. Add jitter to the cooldown across instances so a fleet of servers does not synchronise its probes into a periodic thundering herd — a genuinely production-flavoured detail. WHAT TO DO WHEN THE BREAKER IS OPEN is the other half of the value, and a breaker without a plan for rejection just converts slow failures into fast ones. Options, in rough order of preference: serve a CACHED or stale value (a slightly old recommendation list is better than none); serve a DEGRADED default (hide the personalised section, show the generic one); QUEUE the work for later if it is asynchronous and the operation tolerates delay (a notification, not a payment authorization); or FAIL FAST with a clear error, which is legitimately the right answer for operations that must not be faked — you cannot fall back on a payment. The critical framing is that fail-fast is already a large win even with no fallback, because the caller's threads are freed instantly instead of parking for a three-second timeout each, and thread-pool exhaustion is what turns one dependency's outage into your outage. Pair the breaker with a BULKHEAD (a bounded pool of concurrent calls per dependency) so that even in CLOSED state a slow dependency can only consume a fixed slice of your capacity, and note the ISOLATION rule: one breaker PER DEPENDENCY (and often per endpoint), keyed in a registry, because a global breaker means a failing recommendation service blocks payments — coupling that inverts the entire point of the pattern."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "COUNT-BASED vs TIME-BASED WINDOW: last-N-calls is simple, allocation-free, and ideal for steady traffic; per-second buckets over the last M seconds handle bursty and low-volume traffic where the last hundred calls may span an hour of irrelevant history, at the cost of more bookkeeping and a bucket-rotation subtlety. THRESHOLD TUNING is a real trade with no universal answer, and saying so is better than inventing numbers: a low failure-rate threshold with a short window trips fast and flaps on transient blips; a high threshold with a long window is stable but leaves you calling a dead dependency for longer. Typical starting points are fifty percent over a hundred calls with a minimum of twenty, and a thirty-second open duration — presented as starting points to be tuned from observed data, not as truth. LOCAL vs DISTRIBUTED STATE: a per-instance breaker means each of fifty servers must independently discover the outage, so fifty times the minimum-call volume is wasted on a dead dependency; a shared breaker state (in Redis, say) makes the fleet trip together but introduces a network call on the hot path, a new dependency that itself can fail, and a coordination problem — the near-universal production choice is LOCAL state, accepting the redundant discovery, because a breaker that depends on a remote store to decide whether remote stores are healthy is circular. Say that explicitly; it is exactly the kind of judgment being graded. BREAKER vs RETRY vs BULKHEAD vs TIMEOUT: they solve different problems and compose — timeout bounds one call, retry handles a transient blip, the bulkhead bounds concurrent resource consumption, and the breaker handles a sustained outage. Retry WITHOUT a breaker amplifies an outage (every client multiplying load against a struggling service is the classic retry storm), so the two belong together with the breaker outside. WHAT COUNTS AS A FAILURE deserves explicit configuration: HTTP 500 and timeouts yes, HTTP 400 and business exceptions no — a breaker that opens because users are submitting invalid input has stopped a healthy dependency, and this misconfiguration is common enough in real systems to be worth naming. Extensions to expect: exponential backoff with jitter on repeated failed probes; forced states for operators (manually open a breaker to shed load during an incident, or force closed to override a false trip); per-endpoint rather than per-service granularity when one slow endpoint should not gate the rest; adaptive thresholds derived from baseline error rates; and rich metrics — state transitions, rejection counts, and window rate — since a breaker that opens invisibly turns a dependency outage into an unexplained feature outage."),
      C("deep_dive", "Common follow-ups & how it evolves",
        "Expect: 'Name the states and every legal transition.' — CLOSED to OPEN on rate threshold with minimum volume; OPEN to HALF_OPEN lazily after cooldown; HALF_OPEN to CLOSED on a successful probe; HALF_OPEN to OPEN on a failed probe. No CLOSED to HALF_OPEN, and no OPEN to CLOSED without a probe. 'Why not count consecutive failures?' — it misses a dependency failing fifty percent of the time and falsely trips on a low-volume path; use a failure RATE over a sliding window gated by a minimum call count. 'One failure in three calls trips your breaker?' — that is what the minimum-calls gate prevents; a rate over a sample too small to be meaningful must not be trusted. 'A thousand threads hit the breaker the instant the cooldown expires.' — admission is compareAndSet(OPEN, HALF_OPEN): exactly one CAS succeeds and probes, everyone else is rejected. A boolean flag fails because two threads read it as false before either writes. 'Why not a background thread to flip OPEN to HALF_OPEN?' — lazily transitioning on the next arriving call needs no thread per breaker and probes exactly when traffic exists. 'What about a call that succeeds in nine seconds?' — count it as a failure via the slow-call threshold; latency exhausts your pool just as effectively as errors. 'What happens to the window when state changes?' — reset on open and on close, so pre-outage outcomes cannot flap a recovered dependency. 'What do callers do when it is open?' — cached value, degraded default, queue for later, or fast failure; fast failure alone is already a large win because threads stop parking on timeouts. 'One breaker for the whole service?' — no: one per dependency (often per endpoint) via a registry, or a failing recommendation service blocks payments. 'Fifty instances each learn separately?' — yes, and that is the accepted trade; shared state in Redis puts a network dependency on the hot path of the component whose job is surviving network dependencies. 'Breaker and retry together?' — breaker outside, retry inside a timeout, so an open breaker rejects the whole operation once instead of consuming N retries against a dead dependency."),
    ],
    "The circuit breaker is the resilience pattern most likely to appear in a senior LLD loop, and it is graded on precision rather than familiarity. Interviewers expect the three-state machine enumerated exactly, including the transitions that must NOT exist, and then push on two mechanisms: the detector — where a consecutive-failure counter is the wrong answer and a sliding-window failure rate with a minimum-throughput gate is the right one — and HALF_OPEN admission, which is a compare-and-set problem, since a boolean 'probe in flight' flag is read as free by every thread that hits the cooldown boundary simultaneously and releases the exact stampede the breaker exists to prevent. Strong candidates also volunteer slow calls as failures, window reset on transition, per-dependency isolation via a registry, the composition order with retry and timeout, and why fleet-wide shared breaker state is usually the wrong trade.",
    [
      G("consecutive_failure_counter", "Tripping the breaker after N consecutive failures, with no rate and no minimum call volume.", "Use a failure RATE over a sliding window (ring buffer with running counters, O(1) per record) and require a minimum number of calls before the rate is trusted; count slow calls above a latency threshold as failures too.", "an int failureCount reset to zero on any success; a breaker that never opens while half the calls error; a breaker that opens after the first error on a rarely-used path; a window scanned on every call."),
      G("halfopen_boolean_flag", "Admitting the recovery probe with a boolean flag or a plain state read, letting many threads pass simultaneously when the cooldown expires.", "Make admission a compare-and-set on the state — compareAndSet(OPEN, HALF_OPEN) — so exactly one thread wins and all others are rejected; for N probes use an atomic permit counter, never a flag.", "if (!probeInFlight) { probeInFlight = true; ... }; a surge of traffic against the dependency the instant the breaker cools down; volatile used as if it provided atomicity; HALF_OPEN allowing unlimited calls."),
      G("global_breaker_no_fallback", "One breaker shared across all dependencies, counting business exceptions as failures, with nothing defined for the open state and no window reset on transition.", "Key breakers per dependency (often per endpoint) in a registry, configure a predicate so only dependency failures and timeouts count, define the fallback (cached, degraded, queued, or fail fast), and reset the window on open and close.", "a single static CircuitBreaker instance; the breaker opening because users submitted invalid input; open state throwing a raw exception the caller does not handle; immediate re-opening (flapping) right after a successful close."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.breaker, "Circuit breaker class diagram"),
];

const xpFor = (l) => (l === "warmup" ? 5 : l === "easy" ? 10 : l === "medium" ? 15 : 20);
const diffFor = (l) => (l === "warmup" ? 0.1 : l === "easy" ? 0.2 : l === "medium" ? 0.4 : 0.6);
function pm(moduleId, o) {
  return { trackKey: TRACK_KEY, moduleId, topicId: o.topicId, exerciseId: o.exerciseId,
    position: o.position, level: o.level, type: "pattern_match", title: o.title, scenario: o.scenario,
    instructions: "Pick the best answer.", starterCode: "", expectedSolution: o.correct,
    blanks: [{ options: o.options }], testCases: [{ type: "pattern_match", correct: o.correct, explanation: o.explanation }],
    hints: [], xpReward: xpFor(o.level), difficulty: diffFor(o.level) };
}

